1202:
** δεσμ-ώτης**, ου, ὁ,
**prisoner, captive,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.143, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.35, etc.}}:—_feminine_ **δεσμ-ῶτις**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 8.8}}: _metaphorically_ of the soul, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **in chains, fettered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 119 (the play is called Προμηθεὺς δ.): _feminine_ δεσμῶτις ποίμνη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 234 (Lyric poetry)}}; Μελανίππη δ., name of a play by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}
**III** **gaoler,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 189.}}

1203:
** δεσπότ-ης**, ου, ;
_vocative_ δέσποτᾰ: _Ionic dialect_ _accusative_ δεσπότεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.91, +others, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 25}}:—**master, lord,** properly **the master of the house,** δόμων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 60, etc.; ὄμμα γὰρ δόμων νομίζω δεσπότου παρουσίαν [prev. author] “Pers.” 169}}: _plural_, of a family, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 32, “Ch.” 53, 82 (Lyric poetry)}}; in respect of slaves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 133d; δοῦλοι καὶ δ. οὐκ ἄν ποτε γένοιντο φίλοι [prev. author] “Lg.” 757a, etc.; δ. καὶ δοῦλος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1253b6, compare 1278b35; ὦ δέσποτ᾽ ἄναξ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 90 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὦναξ δέσποτα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 389, “fragment” 598; δέσποτ᾽ ἄναξ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 312.5.}}
**2** **despot, absolute ruler,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.89, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.77; τύραννος καὶ δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 859a}}; of the Roman **Emperors,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.568, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 55.12, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.6.4; γᾶς καὶ θαλάσσας δ. “IG” 12(2).216 (from Mytilene).}}
**3** of the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 535, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 88, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 875, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13.}}
**4** **dominant planet,** {{Refs|Vett. Val. 5.16.}}
**II** generally, **master, lord, owner,** κώμου, ναῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.18, “P.” 4.207; μαντευμάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 27; τῶν Ἡρακλείων ὅπλων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 262}}; ἑπτὰ δεσποτῶν, of the seven {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Chiefs” against Thebes, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 636; τοῦ ὄρτυγος 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.108.—Not in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (for metrical reasons), though he uses δέσποινα in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” (Prob. for δεμσποτ- 'lord of the house', compare δόμος.)}}

1204:
** δεῦρο**
(_Aeolic dialect_ δεῦρυ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.933, who read δεύρω in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.240)}}, strengthened in _Attic dialect_ **δευρί** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 323, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.10: sometimes written δεῦρε}} in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., as {{Refs|“IG” 12.900}}: late **δευρεί** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Stud.Pal.” 10.7.6 (4th-5th c.AD)}}. _adverb_:
**I** of Place, **hither,** with all Verbs of motion, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.153, etc.: strengthened, δ. τόδ᾽ ἵκω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.444, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.309}}; in _pregnant_ sense with Verbs of rest, **to [have come hither and] be here,** δ. παρέστης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3.405; πάρεστι δ... ὅδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1253; τὰ τῇδε καὶ τὰ δεῦρο πάντ᾽ ἀνασκόπει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 666: with _Article_, μακρὸν τὸ δ. πέλαγος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 663}}; τῆς δ. ὁδοῦ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1165; τὸ τῇδε καὶ τὸ κεῖσε καὶ τὸ δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 426, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 266, [315]; δ. ἐλθών 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 143a.}}
**I.b** later, **here,** τὰ δ., = **sensible objects,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 991b30; τὰ σώματα τὰ δ. [prev. author] “Cael.” 269b15; τὰ δ. κακά 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 14.7.}}
**I.2** used as Interjection, **come on!** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus with _2nd pers._ _sing._ _imperative_ (δεῦτε (which see) being used with _plural_), ἄγε δ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.314; δ. ἄγε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.145; δ. ἴθι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.130; δ. ἴτω 7.75; δ. ὄρσο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.395}}: later with _2nd pers._ _plural_ _imperative_, δ. ἴτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1041 (Lyric poetry); δ. ἕπεσθε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 724.}}
**I.2.b** with _1st pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_, δεῦρο, φίλη, λέκτρονδε τραπείομεν **come** let us.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.292, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.120, +others}}; later in this sense with _imperative_, καί μοι δ. εἰπέ **here now,** tell me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 24c}}; δεῦρό σου στέψω κάρα **come** let me.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 341.}}
**I.2.c** without a Verb, δ. δηὖτε Μοῖσαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 84}}; δεῦρο, σύ **here,** you! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 881}}; δ. παρὰ Σωκράτη (i.e. καθίζου) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144d}}; δ. δὴ πάλιν (i.e. βλέπε) {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 477d.}}
**I.2.d** later, **go away!** {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.3.13.}}
**I.3** in arguments, μέχρι δ. τοῦ λόγου up **to this point** of the argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217e; τὸ μέχρι δ. ἡμῖν εἰρήσθω [prev. author] “Lg.” 814d; δεῦρ᾽ ἀεὶ προεληλύθαμεν [prev. author] “Plt.” 292c; ἄχρι δ. 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.453.}}
**II** of Time, **until now, hitherto,** Trag. (see. below) and Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21d; μέχρι τοῦ δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64, 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus “Praef.” 7, “PLond.” 2.358.16 (2nd c.AD)}}; μέχριδεύρου (sic) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGen.” 47.8 (4th c.AD)}}; εἰς τὴν δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 1.19; ἐξ ἕω μέχρι δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 811c; δεῦρ᾽ ἀεί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 670, “Ion” 56, etc.; _paratragoedia_ in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1135; δεῦρό γ᾽ ἀεί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 596.}}

1208:
** δεύτερος**, α, ον,
**second,** (perhaps from δύο with _comparative_ termination):
**I** **next** in Order (with a notion of Time), in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” (not in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”)}} of one who **comes in second** in a race,{{Refs|23.265; δ. ἐλθεῖν 22.207; δ. αὖτ᾽.. προΐει.. ἔγχος}}**next,** {{Refs|20.273, etc.}}; οὔ μ᾽ ἔτι δ. ὧδε ἵξετ᾽ ἄχος no **second** grief, i.e. none **hereafter** like this, {{Refs|23.46}}; as _comparative_, with _genitive_, ἐμεῖο δεύτεροι **after** my **time,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 248}}; σοὶ δ᾽ οὐκέτι δ. ἔσται no **second choice** will be allowed thee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 34}}; in _Attic dialect_ and Trag. with _Article_, ὁ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1315, etc.}}; αἱ δ. πως φροντίδες σοφώτεραι **second** thoughts are wisest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 436: proverbial, δ. πλοῦς}} the **next best** way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99d, etc.; ὁ δ. πλοῦς ἐστι δήπου λεγόμενος, ἂν ἀποτύχῃ τις οὐρίου, κώπαισι πλεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 241.}}
**I.2** of Time, **next, later,** δ. χρόνῳ in **after** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.43}}; δ. ἡμέρῃ on the **next** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82}}; δ. ἔτεϊ τούτων in the year **after** this, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.46: _neuter_ as}} _adverb_, δεύτερον αὖ, αὖτε, αὖτις, **a second time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.332, 191, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.354; ἐν τᾷ δ. ἐκκλησίᾳ “SIG” 644.20}}: with the _Article_, τὸ δ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 4.11, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1082, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.1: also _plural_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53, 9.3}}; τὰ δ. κινδυνεύσοντας about to run the **next** dangers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.78}}; later, ἐκ δευτέρου for **the second time,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Mark.14.72, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.87.10; ἐκ δευτέρης 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 114.5, compare “PStrassb.” 100.22 (2nd c.BC)}}: regular _adverb_ δευτέρως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 955e, 4th c.AD: Sallustius Philosophus 18, etc.}}
**I.2.b** ὁ δ. the **younger**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 592.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** in Order or Rank (without any notion of Time), **second,** δ.μετ᾽ ἐκεῖνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1442, etc.; πολὺ δ. [prev. author] “OC” 1228 (Lyric poetry)}}; πολὺ δ. μετά τι very much **behind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97; μετὰ τὸ πλουτεῖν δ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 144.9: with _genitive_, δ. οὐδενός}} **second** to none, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.23, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.27.16; δ. παιδὸς σῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 618; πάντα τἄλλα δεύτερ᾽ ἦν τῶν προσδοκιῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.24; πρὸς τὰ χρήματα θνητοῖσι τἄλλα δεύτερ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 354.5; τὰ ἄλλα πάντα δ. τε καὶ ὕστερα λεκτέον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 59c}}; logically or metaphysically **posterior,** πᾶν πλῆθος δ. ἐστι τοῦ ἑνός {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 5, compare 36, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 126, +others}}; δεύτερ᾽ ἡγεῖσθαι think **quite secondary,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 351}}; δεύτερον ἄγειν, δεύτερα ποιεῖσθαι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.162e; ἐν δευτέρῳ τίθεσθαι [prev. author] “Fab.” 24, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 8.242b}}; ἱερὸν δ. **of the second class**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.59 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.2** **the second of two,** δ. αὐτή herself **with another,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.113, compare “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 89}}; ἑπτὰ δ. σοφοί a **second** seven sages, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 1.12}}; εἷς καὶ δ. **unus et alter,**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Hdn.Gr. 2.934; εἷς ἢ δ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.190d; ἕν τι.. ἢ δεύτερον 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 33.7}}; δ. καὶ τρίτος **two** or three, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 26.1.1.}}; _neuter_ as _adverb_, ἅπαξ καὶ δεύτερον once or **twice,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}} **ad Ath.**278c.
**II.3** δ. ἀριθμός number **whose prime factors are odd,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.12.}}
**III** as _substantive_, τὰ δ., ={δευτερεῖα}, **the second prize** or **place,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.538; τὰ δ. φέρεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.104.}}
**III.2** **after-birth,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.48,50.}}
**III.3** δευτέρα σαββάτου (i.e. ἡμέρα) **second day of the week,** {{LXX|LXX.Psa.47(48) at Title Page}}

1209:
** δέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_, Cretan dialect **δέκομαι**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.91, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.22, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.69}}, _imperfect_ ἐδεκόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.135:}}_future_ δέξομαι, _Epic dialect_ also δεδέξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.238, also in “Anthologia Graeca” 5.8 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 28(49).24; δεξοῦμαι “SIG” 360.29 (from Chersonesus)}}; δεχθήσομαι (in _passive_ sense) {{LXX|LXX.Lev.22.25}}: _aorist_ ἐδεξάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.238, etc., δεξάμην 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.70}}; also ἐδέχθην (ὑπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 757 (Lyric poetry, δεχθείς in _passive_sense), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.6.4, (εἰσ-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.14 (_passive_)}}: _perfect_ δέδεγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.107, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.100, etc.}}; _imperative_ δεδεξο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.228, _plural_ δέδεχθε “hymnus ad Apollinem” 538}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἀπο-δεδέχαται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.43, +others:— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} also has _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἐδέγμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.513}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ δέκτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.88, +others, later ἔδεκτο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.54, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 184; _imperative_ δέξο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.10, _plural_ δέχθε 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1554}}; _infinitive_ δέχθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 525}}; _participle_ δέγμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.524 (also δέχμενος 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; also a _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _present_ δέχαται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.147}}; compare προτίδεγμαι, and see at {δεδοκημένος}:—
**I** of things as the object, **take, accept, receive,** etc., ἄποινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.20, etc.; μισθὸν τῆς φυλακῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 416e; φόρον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90; δ. τι χείρεσσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.355; τὸ διδόμενον παρά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 499c; τι ἐν παρακαταθήκῃ παρά τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 33.6.2, etc.}}; δ. τί τινι **receive** something **at the hand of** another, δέξατό οἱ σκῆπτρον πατρώϊον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.186, compare “IG” 12(3).1075(Melos, 6th c.BC), etc.}}; **accept** as legal tender, ὀδελός {{Refs|“GDI” 5011 (from Gortyn)}}; τι παρά τινος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.429; τι ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1107 (Lyric poetry); τί τινος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.596, 24.305, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1163}}; also δ. τί τινος **receive in exchange for..,** χρυσὸν φίλου ἀνδρὸς ἐδέξατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.327}}; **choose,** τι δ. πρό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729d; μᾶλλον δ. τι ἀντί τινος [prev. author] “Grg.” 475d}}: with _infinitive_, **prefer,** δεξαίμην ἂν πάσας τὰς ἀσπίδας ἐρριφέναι ἢ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 63b; δ. μᾶλλον.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.14, “Smp.” 4.12; οὐδεὶς ἂν δέξαιτο φεύγειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.143; Ὀρφεῖ συγγενέσθαι ἐπὶ πόσῳ ἄν τις δέξαιτ᾽ ἂν ὑμῶ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 41a; οὐκ ἂν δεξαίμην τι ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.5.}}
**I.b** **catch,** as in a vessel, ὀπὸν.. κάδοις δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 534.3.}}
**I.2** of mental reception, **take, accept** without complaint, χαλεπόν περ ἐόντα δεχώμεθα μῦθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.271; κῆρα δ᾽ ἐγὼ τότε δέξομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.115.}}
**I.2.b** **accept** graciously, τοῦτο δ᾽ ἐγὼ πρόφρων δ. {{Refs|23.647}}; of the gods, ἀλλ᾽ ὅ γε δέκτο μὲν ἱρά {{Refs|2.420}}; προσφιλῶς γέρα δ., of one dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 443; τὰ σφάγια δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 204, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.86}}; τὸ χρησθέν, τὸν οἰωνὸν δ., **accept, hail** the oracle, the omen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, 9.91; δέχου τὸν ἄνδρα καὶ τὸν ὄρνιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 63; δ. τὰ ἀγαθά “IG” 22.410, +others; ἐδεξάμην τὸ ῥηθέν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 668: _absolutely_, δεχομένοις λέγεις θανεῖν σε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1653, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.17}}; **accept, approve,** τὸν λόγον, ξυμμαχίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.5, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37; τοὺς λόγους [prev. work] 95}}; διδόναι καὶ δέχεσθαι τὰ δίκαια{{Refs|[prev. work] 37, compare “hymnus ad Mercurium” 312}}; δέχεσθαι ὅρκον, see at {ὅρκος}; **accept** a confession, and so **forgive,** ἀδικίαν {{LXX|LXX.Gen.50.17.}}
**I.2.c** simply, **give ear to, hear,** ὠσὶν ἠχήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1086; δ. ὀμφάν [prev. author] “Med.” 175 (Lyric poetry); τὰ παραγγελλόμενα ὀξέως δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, 89.}}
**I.2.d** **take** or **regard** as so and so, μηδὲ συμφορὰν δέχου τὸν ἄνδρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 68}}; **understand** in a certain sense, ὅπῃ βούλει δέξασθαι ταύτῃ δέχου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 315c}}: with _infinitive_, κῶλά με δέξαι νυνὶ λέγειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.13, etc.}}
**I.2.e** **cap** verses, σκόλια δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1222.}}
**I.3** **take upon oneself,** τὴν δαπάνην {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.28.5}}: with _infinitive_, **undertake**, {{Refs|“SIG” 245.34.}}
**II** of persons as the object, **welcome,** κόλπῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.483; ἀγαθῷ νόῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60}}; ἐν μεγάροισι, ἐν δόμοισιν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.331, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.110; δόμοις δ. τινά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 818}}; στέγαις, πυρὶ δ. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 47; δ. χώρᾳ [prev. author] “Med.” 713}}; τῇ τόλει δ. **to admit** into the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.103}}; ἀγορᾷ, ἄστει δ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.44; ἔσω [prev. passage]; εἰς τὸ τεῖχος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.6}}; δ. τινὰ ξύμμαχον **accept** or **admit** as an ally, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.43, etc.}}; **accept as security,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|PGrenf. 1.33.4, etc.: _metaphorically_ of places, τόποι τοὺς κατοικιζομένους ἵλεῳ δεχόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 747e}}; **entertain,** δείπνοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 41.2 (_anapaest meter_); δωρήμασιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 4.}}
**II.2** **receive as an enemy, await the attack of,** ἐπιόντα δ. δουρί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.238, compare 15.745}}; of a hunter **waiting for** game, {{Refs|4.107}}; of a wild boar **waiting for** the hunters, {{Refs|12.147}}; of troops, εἰς χεῖρας δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.31; τοὺς Λακεδαιμονίους δ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.54, compare 8.28, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.43; ἐπιόντας δ. [prev. author] 7.77; δ. τὴν πρώτην ἔφοδον [prev. author] 4.126; ἐδέξατο πόλις πόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 393.}}
**II.3** **expect, wait,** with _accusative_ et _future_ _infinitive_, ἀλλ᾽ αἰεί τινα φῶτα.. ἐδέγμην ἐνθάδ᾽ ἐλεύσεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.513, compare 12.230; also δέγμενος Αἰακίδην, ὁπότε λήξειεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.191; δεδεγμένος εἰσόκεν ἔλθῃς 10.62.}}—In these two last senses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always uses}}_future_ δεδέξομαι, _perfect_ δέδεγμαι, and δεδεγμένος,{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|compare δεδεγμένος ὁππόθ᾽ ἵκοιτο 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.228; δέγμενος is used in sense 3 only, except in “hymnus ad Cererem” 29, “Merc.” 477:}}_infinitive_ δειδέχθαι as _imperative_, **expect,** with _genitive_, βορέω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 795, compare 907, 928.}}
**III** rarely with a thing as the subject, **occupy, engage** one, τίς ἀρχὰ δέξατο ναυτιλίας [αὐτούς]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.70.}}
**III.2** **receive, hold,** τὴν τροφήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 531a23, +others; οἰκίαι ἱκαναὶ δέξασθαι ὑμᾶς “SIG” 344.10.}}
**III.3** **admit of,** ψεῦδος οὐδὲν δ. ἁ τῶ ἀριθμῶ φύσις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 11; τὸ μᾶλλον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 146a3, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 2.}}
**III.4** Geometry texts, **contain, circum-scribe,** γωνίας ἴσας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 3 “Def.” 11; πεντάγωνον 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 422.34.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, **succeed, come next,** ὥς μοι δέχεται κακὸν ἐκ κακοῦ αἰεί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.290; ἄλλος γ᾽ ἐξ ἄλλου δέχεται χαλεπώτερος ἆθλος 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 800; ἄλλος ἐξ ἄλλου δ. 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 115.12}}; of places, ἐκ τοῦ στεινοῦ τὸ Ἀρτεμίσιον δέκεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176. (δέκομαι}} is probably the original form, cf. Slavonic _desiti, dositi_ 'find'.)

1210:
** δέω**,
**B** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1006, etc.}}: _future_ δεήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395e}}: _aorist_ ἐδέησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.8, _Epic dialect_ δῆσα only 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.100}}: _perfect_ δεδέηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277d}}:—_middle_, _future_ δεήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ δεοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 120}}; later **-ηθήσομαι** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Job.5.8, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.213c, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐδεήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 986, etc.}}: _perfect_ δεδέημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.14, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.22 (the forms δεήσω, etc., compared with the _Epic dialect_ ἐδεύησα, δεύομαι, point to root δεϝ)}}:—**lack, miss, stand in need of,** with _genitive_, ἐμεῖο δὲ δῆσε.. ἀλκτῆρα γενέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [prev. cited] (elsewhere 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses δεύω, which see)}}; παραδείγματος τὸ παράδειγμα αὐτὸ δεδέηκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 277d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.10.}}
**B.2** frequently in _Attic dialect_, πολλοῦ δέω I **want** much, i.e. **am** far **from,** mostly with _infinitive_ _present_, πολλοῦ δ. ἀπολογεῖσθαι I **am** far **from** defending myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30d; πολλοῦ δεῖς εἰπεῖν [prev. author] “Men.” 79b; π. δ. ἀγνοεῖν [prev. author] “Ly.” 204e; π. γε δέουσι μαίνεσθαι [prev. author] “Men.” 92a; also μικροῦ ἔδεον ἐν χερσὶν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.6.11, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 25; τοσούτου δέω ἱκανὸς εἶναι λέγειν ὥστε.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.1; τοσούτου δέουσι μιμεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.17 (also τοσοῦτον δέω εἰδέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71a)}}; παρὰ μικρὸν ἐδέησα ἀποθανεῖν variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.42; simply ἐδέησα κινδύνῳ περιπεσεῖν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.5: _absolutely_, πολλοῦ γε δέω}} I **am** far **from** it, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a; τοῦ παντὸς δέω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1006; παντὸς δεῖ τοιοῦτος εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 221d (_impersonal_ πολλοῦ δεῖ, etc., see at {δεῖ} II.1. b)}}]: in _participle_, παλαστῆς δεόντων τεττάρων ποδῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.373.8; μικροῦ δέοντα τέτταρα τάλαντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.35}}; the _participle_ is frequently used to express numerals compounded with 8 or 9, ἀνδράσιν ἑνὸς δέουσι τριάκοντα {{Refs|“IG” 12.374.413}}; δυοῖν δέοντα τεσσεράκοντα forty **lacking** two, thirty-eight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14; πεντήκοντα δυοῖν δέοντα ἔτη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2}}; ἑνὸς δέον εἰκοστὸν ἔτος the {{Refs|20th year}}**save** one,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|the 19th, [prev. author] 8.6; δυοῖν δεούσαις εἴκοσι ναυσίν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.5: later, the}}_infinitive_ stands _absolutely_, περὶ τὰ ἑνὸς δεῖν πεντήκοντα fifty **save** one, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390b11}}: _participle_ in _genitive_, τροφαλίδες μιᾶς δεούσης εἴκοσιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “HA” 522a31; πόλεων δυοῖν δεούσαιν ἑξήκοντα 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.27; ἑξήκοντα ἑνὸς δέοντος ἔτη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 79.}}
**B.3** _participle_ δέων, δέουσα, as _adjective_, **fit, proper,** ὁ καιρὸς οὐκ ἔστι χρόνος δέων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 48b36; τοῖς δέουσι χρόνοις “IG” 12(3).247.11 (from Anaphe)}}; ἡ δέουσα ἑκάστων χρῆσις Hierocl.p.61 [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, etc.: especiallyfrequently in _neuter_, see at {δέον}.}}]
**B.4** δεῖ _impersonal_, see entry
**B.II** _deponent verb_ **δέομαι**: _contraction_ δῆσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}, _participle_ δεύμενος {{Refs|[prev. author] 36}}: _future_ δεήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 53b:}}_aorist_ ἐδεήθην: always personal, and used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in form δεύομαι (see. δεύω B):}}
**B.II.1** _absolutely_, **to be in want** or **need, require,** mostly in _participle_, κάρτα δεόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.59}}; οἱ δεόμενοι the **needy,** opposed to οἱ κεκτημένοι τὰς οὐσίας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.67.}}
**B.II.1.b** **stand in need of, want,** with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, etc.; τὰ σὰ δεῖται κολαστοῦ.. ἔπη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1148; ῥώμης τινὸς δ. [prev. work] 1293}}; οὐδὲν δεῖσθαι τροφῆς **have** no **need of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.43}}; ἤν τι δέωνται βασιλέως if **they have** any **need** of him, {{Refs|[prev. work] 37: with}}_infinitive_, τοῦτο ἔτι δέομαι μαθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 392d, compare “Euthd.” 275d, etc.}}; τὰ πράττεσθαι δεόμενα **things needing** to be done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.3; τὰ δεόμενα}}**necessaries,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 2.573.4}}; ἐπισκευάσαι τὰ δεόμενα parts **needing** repair, {{Refs|[prev. work]22.1176.15}}; τὸ δεόμενον **the point threatened,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.15.7}}; δεῖται _impersonal_, see at {δεῖ}.
**B.II.2** **beg** a thing **from** a person, with _double_ _genitive_ _of things_ et _person_, τῶν ἐδέετο σφέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.157, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, etc.; μή μου δεηθῇς. ΘΗ. πράγματος ποίο; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1170: frequently with _neuter_ _pronoun_ in _accusative_, τοῦτο ὑμῶν δέομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c, compare “Smp.” 173e, etc.: with _accusative_ _cognate_, δέημα, or oftener δέησιν, δεῖσθαί τινος, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1059, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.43, etc.: also with _accusative_ _of things_ only, ξύμφορα δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32; δυνατά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335e; δίκαια καὶ μέτρια ὑμῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.2; διαπράξωμαι ἃ δέομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.29: with _genitive_ _person_ only, δεηθεὶς ὑμῶν}} **having begged a favour** of you, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.108}}: c._genitive_ _person_ et _infinitive_, ἐδέετο τοῦ δήμου φυλακῆς πρὸς αὐτοῦ κυρῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336a, etc.; δ. τινὸς ὥστε.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.119; ὅπως.. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 84: rarely with _accusative_ _person_, ἐδέοντο Βοιωτοὺς ὅπως παραδώσουσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.36: parenthetic, δέομαι}}**I pray,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.44.18.}}

1211:
** δή**,
properly a temporal Particle (compare ἤδη), **at this** or **that point**: hence, **now, then, already,** or **at length**:
**I** in _Epic dialect_ (rarely Lyric poetry) sometimes at the beginning of a sentence or clause, Τεῦκρε πέπον, δὴ νῶϊν ἀπέκτατο πιστὸς ἑταῖρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.437; δὴ πάμπαν ἀποίχεαι ἀνδρὸς ἑῆος 19.342; δὴ γὰρ μέγα νεῖκος ὄρωρεν 13.122; δὴ τότε, δή ῥα τότε, 1.476, 13.719, +others,compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.25, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 214 (Lyric poetry): but usually second (or nearly so)}}, frequently with Numerals and temporal Particles, ὀκτὼ δὴ προέηκα.. ὀϊστούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.297}}; ἐννέα δὴ βεβάασι.. ἐνιαυτοί **full** nine years, {{Refs|2.134}}; ἕκτον δὲ δὴ τόδ᾽ ἦμαρ this is **just** the sixth day, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 39, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.107, etc.: also after _adverbs_ of Time, πολλάκι δή}} many a time **and oft,** often **ere now,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|19.85; ὀψὲ δὲ δή 7.94; τρὶς δή 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.91}}; πάλαι δή, Latin _jamdudum,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 806}}; νῦν δή **just** now, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 923 (frequently written νυνδή, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 145b, etc.); νῦν τε καὶ ἄλλοτε δή [prev. work] 187d}}; now **at length,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 353a, etc.}}; τότε δή at that **very** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49, etc.}}; αὐτίκα δὴ μάλα this **very** instant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 338b, etc.}}; ὕστερον δή **yet** later, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17: frequently with temporal Conjunctions, ἐπεὶ δή (written ἐπειδή, which see), etc.}}
**II** without temporal significance, as a Particle of emphasis, **in fact, of course, certainly,** ναὶ δή, ἦ δή, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.286, 518, etc.}}; οὐ δή **surely** not, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 246, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1069, etc.}}; δῆλα δή, see at {δῆλος}; with Verbs, δὴ γὰρ ἴδον ὀφθαλμοῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.488; νῦν δὲ ὁρᾶτε δή 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.12; καὶ ἴστε δὴ οἷος.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a: less frequently with Substantives, σοφιστὴν δή τοι ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι}} they call the man a sophist **as you know,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 311e: with Conjunctions, ἵνα δή, ὡς δή, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.207, 5.24, etc.; ὅπως δή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.85}}; γὰρ δή for **manifestly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 874, 891, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 156c; οὐ γὰρ δή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 265: hence with a}}_participle_ representing Conjunction and Verb, ἅτε δὴ ἐόντες since they **evidently** are, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.90}}; but ὡς φόνον νίζουσα δή **as though** she were.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1338, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.4, etc.; and so, ironically, ὡς δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.110, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1315, “Eq.” 693, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342c, +others; frequently with σύ, ὡς δὴ σύ μοι τύραννος Ἀργείων ἔσῃ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1633, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 809, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 235, etc.}}; also ἵνα δή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420e, “Men.” 86d; ὅτι δή.. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 268d}}; also εἰσήγαγε τὰς ἑταιρίδας δή the **pretended** courtesans, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.6, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1181, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67, 6.80.}}
**II.2** frequently placed immediately after Pronouns, ἐμὲ δή me **of all persons,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.155}}; σὺ δή you **of all persons,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.115, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1226}}; οὗτος δή this **and no other,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.43; ὑμεῖς δὲ κεῖνοι δὴ οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1091}}; οὗτος δὴ ὁ Σωκράτης, ironically, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a}}; τὸ λεγόμενον δὴ τοῦτο as the **well-known** saying goes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 514e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 962}}; δή τις some one **you know of,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 108c, +others: with possess. Pronouns, τὸ σὸν δὴ τοῦτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 221b, compare “Grg.” 5c8 d, etc.: with relatives, ὃς δὴ νῦν κρατέει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.315; τὰ δὴ καὶ ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; οἷος δὴ σύ **just** such as thou, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.376, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.32, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 995, etc.; ὅσα δή 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1, etc.: with _adjectives_, οἴη δή, μοῦνος δή, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.69, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.25; ἐν πολλῇ δὴ ἀπορίᾳ ἦσαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.2: frequently with Superlatives, μάχη ἐγένετο πλείστου δὴ χρόνου μεγίστη δὴ τῶν Ἑλληνικῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.74; ἁπάντων δὴ ἄλγιστον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 992, etc.}}
**III** to mark a transition, with or without inference, **so, then,** νίκη μὲν δὴ φαίνετ᾽.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.457; τὴν μὲν δὴ τυραννίδα οὕτω ἔσχον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14; τοῦτο δὴ τὸ ἄγος οἱ Λακεδαιμόνιοι ἐκέλευον ἐλαύνειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.127.}}
**IV** with Indef. Particles, see at {δήποθεν, δήποτε, δήπω, δήπουθεν}: with interrogatives, τοῦ δὴ ἕνεκ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 457e; τί δὲ δή..; [prev. author] “Phd.” 58c}} (simply τί δ; what **then? R.**{{Refs|357d)}}; πότερα δ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1235 (and with _adverbs_, ποῖ δὴ καὶ πόθε; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.”}}[near the start]; ποῦ δ; πῇ δ;{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 228e, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.339, etc.): with Indef. Pronouns, δή strengthens the _indefinite_ notion, ἄλλοισιν δὴ ταῦτ᾽ ἐπιτέλλεο}} others **be they who they may,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.295}}; μηδεὶς δή no one **at all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170e}}; δή τις some one **or other,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 498a (_plural_), etc. (rarely τις δή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 946)}}; the _neuter_ δή τι is common, ἦ ἄρα δή τι ἐΐσκομεν ἄξιον εἶνα; in any way, **whatever it be,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.446; τὸ ἱππικόν, τῷ δή τι καὶ ἐπεῖχε ἐλλάμψεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80; οὕτω δή τι [prev. author] 3.108, etc.; θεῶν ὅτεῳ δή}}**whosoever it be,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.86}}; ἐπὶ μισθῷ ὅσῳ δή, Latin _quantocumque,_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 160}}, etc.; οἵα δή γε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 632, compare “Supp.” 162; but θαυμαστὰ δὴ ὅσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220b; ὡς δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.24, etc.}}; so almost, ={ἤδη, ἀναπέτομαι δὴ πρὸς Ὄλυμπον} {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 24; καὶ δὴ φίλον τις ἔκταν᾽ ἀγνοίας ὕπο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 499; ἤκουσα δή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 823; πάθη μὲν οὖν δὴ πόλλ᾽ ἔγωγ᾽ ἐκλαυσάμην [prev. author] “Tr.” 153; οἶσθα μὲν δή [prev. work] 627}}; so καὶ δή **already, in fact,** frequently not at the beginning of the sentence, κεῖται καὶ δὴ πάνθ᾽ ἅπερ εἶπας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 514, compare “Nu.” 906, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.83; but καὶ δή σφε λείπω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 507.}}
**IV.2** to continue a narrative, frequently after μέν, **then, so,** τότε μὲν δὴ.. ἡσυχίην εἶχε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11; Σόλων μὲν δὴ ἔνεμε [prev. work] 32; τὸν μὲν δὴ πέμπει [prev. work] 116; alone, εἷς δὴ τούτων.. ?? one of these.., [prev. work] 114, etc.: frequently in summing up, τοιαῦτα μὲν δὴ ταῦτα}} Latin _haec hactenus,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 500, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4; τούτων δὴ ἕνεκα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.28, etc.}}; in summing up numbers, γίγνονται δὴ οὗτοι χίλιοι these **then** amount to {{Refs|1, 000, [prev. work] 1.5.5}}; in resuming after a parenthesis, Ἀνδρομάχη, θυγάτηρ μεγαλήτορος Ἠετίωνος.., τοῦ περ δὴ θυγάτηρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.395; οὗτος δὴ.., ὁ μὲν δή 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.43.}}
**IV.2.b** with _imperative_ and _subjunctive_, μὴ δὴ.. ἐπιέλπεο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.545, compare 5.684, etc.; χωρῶμεν δὴ πάντες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1469}}; ἐννοεῖτε γὰρ δή for do **but** consider, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.5}}; ἄγε δή, φέρε δή, ἴθι δή, σκόπει δή, λέγε δή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 235a, “Phd.” 63b, “Sph.” 224c, “Phd.” 80a, “Prt.” 312c.}}
**IV.3** to express what follows **a fortiori,** καὶ μετὰ ὅπλων γε δή **above all** with arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.78}}; μή τί γε δή not to mention, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.23; εἰ δὲ δὴ πόλεμος ἥξει [prev. author] 1.27.}}
**IV.4** καὶ δή and **what is more,** adding an emphatic statement, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.161, 15.251, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.4; in Prose, frequently καὶ δὴ καί.., ἐς Αἴγυπτον ἀπίκετο.., καὶ δὴ καὶ ἐς Σάρδις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, etc.}}; καὶ δὴ καὶ νῦν τί φῄ; and **now** what do you say? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187c}}; καὶ δὴ μὲν οὖν παρόντα yes, and **actually** here present, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 31}}; especially in a series, ὑγίεια καὶ ἰσχὺς καὶ κάλλος καὶ πλοῦτος δή and **of course** riches, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 87e, compare “Tht.” 159c, “R.” 367d; εἴτ᾽.. εἴτ᾽.. εἴτεδή [prev. work] 493d.}}
**IV.4.b** καὶ δή is also used in answers, ἦ καὶ παρέστη κἀπὶ τέρμ᾽ ἀφίκετ; Answ. καὶ δὴ 'πὶ δισσαῖς ἦν.. πύλαις **yes,** he was **even** so far as.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 49}}; βλέψον κάτω. Answ. καὶ δὴ βλέπω **well,** I am looking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 175, compare “Pax” 327, “Pl.” 227f, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 317f, 1436, etc.}}; πρόσθιγέ νύν μου. Answ. ψαύω καὶ δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 173}}; without καί, ἀποκρίνου περὶ ὧν ἂν ἐρωτῶ. Answ. ἐρώτα δή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157d}}; ἐρώτα. Answ. ἐρωτῶ δή {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 448b.}}
**IV.4.c** in assumptions or suppositions, καὶ δὴ δέδεγμαι and **now suppose** I have accepted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 894, compare “Ch.” 565, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 386, “Hel.” 1059, not found in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, once in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1224.}}
**IV.5** δή in apodosi, after εἰ or ἐάν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.898, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 524e, etc.}}; after ὅτε, ἡνίκα, **even then,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 170f, “El.” 954}}; after ἐπεί, ἐπειδάν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 435e, etc.}}; after ὡς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.4; ἐν ᾧ δὲ ταῦτα ἐβουλεύοντο, καὶ δὴ ὁ βασιλεύς..}}**already,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.10.10.}}

1212:
** δῆλος**
(also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.79, etc., and _Aeolic dialect_, compare πρόδηλος)}}, η, ον, also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1197}}: _Epic dialect_ **δέελος**:
**I** properly **visible, conspicuous,** δέελον δ᾽ ἐπὶ σῆμά τ᾽ ἔθηκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.466, but:}}
**II** commonly, **clear** to the mind, **manifest,** νῦν δ᾽ ἤδη τόδε δ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.333, etc.}}
**II.2** δ. εἰμι is frequently used with _participle_, δ. ἐστιν ἀλγεινῶς φέρων i.e. **it is clear** that he takes it ill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1011, compare “OT” 673, 1008, etc.}}; οἳ ἂν δ. ὦσι μὴ ἐπιτρέψοντες who are **clearly** not going to permit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71; with ὡς, δ. ἐστιν ὥς τι δρασείων κακόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 326; δ. ἔσεσθε ὡς ὀργιζόμενοι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.9}}; δ. ὁρᾶσθαι.. ὤν being as was **plainly** to be seen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 350: with ὅτι}} and a Verb, δ. ἐστιν ὅτι.. ἀκήκοεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 333; δ. ἡ οἰκοδομία ὅτι κατὰ σπουδὴν ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; δ. ἔσται ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.50: sometimes the}}_participle_ or _relative_ clause must be supplied, καταγελᾷς μου, δ. εἶ (i.e. καταγελῶν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1407, compare [prev. author] “Lys.” 919}}; δῆλοι δέ (i.e. οὐ μένοντες) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10.}}
**II.3** δῆλον ποιεῖν **show plainly,** τινὶ ὅτι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.34, etc.}}: with _participle_, δῆλον ἐποιήσατε.. μηδίσαντες {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.64.}}
**II.4** δῆλον (i.e. ἐστί) **it is manifest,** αὐτὸς πρὸς αὑτοῦ· δῆλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 906; ἀλγεινά, Πρόκνη, δῆλον [prev. author] “fragment” 585; ἐκ πίθω ἀντλεῖς, δῆλον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.13}}; δῆλον δέ, to introduce a proof, followed by γάρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 799a5, etc.; δῆλον γάρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 63; δῆλον ὅτι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38, etc.; τὰ Κύρου δῆλον ὅτι οὕτως ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.9, compare “Cyr.” 2.4.24, etc.}}, see at {δηλονότι}: in _plural_, δῆλα δή, δ. δ. καὶ ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 48b; ἢ δ. δ. ὅτι..; [prev. author] “Prt.” 309a, etc.: hence as}} _adverb_, usually written δηλαδή (which see).
**II.5** _adverb_ δήλως is rejected by _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.207.}}
**III** δῆλοι, οἱ, **Urim,** {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.28.6, +others (Cf. δέατο.)}}

1213:
** δηλ-όω**:—
_passive_, _future_ δηλωθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.144}}; δηλώσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 581; δεδηλώσομαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 45, 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 4}}:—**make visible** or **manifest, show, exhibit,** τὸν ἄνδρ᾽ Ἀχαιοῖς δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 616; ποῖον ὄμμα πατρὶ δηλώσ; [prev. author] “Aj.” 462}}: with _infinitive_ added, ὡς γένος ἄτλητον ἀνθρώποισι δηλώσοιμ᾽ ὁρᾶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 792, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be** or **become manifest,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 581, etc.}}
**2** **make known, disclose, reveal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 519, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 77, etc.}}; **prove,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 146, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3; δηλοῖ ὁ λόγος ὅτι.. 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 7; αὐτὸ δηλώσει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.157}}; **explain, set forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62}}; **signify,** ἐδήλουν οὐδὲν ὅτι ἴσασιν **gave** no **sign** of knowing, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.68}}: **indicate,** τὰς μεγίστας καὶ ἐλαχίστας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.10, etc. Construct.: mostly δ. τινί τι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.30}}; δ. τι πρός or εἴς τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 369, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90; δ. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.7; τινὶ περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.9}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 888.52 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD)}}: followed by a _relative_ clause, δ. ὅτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1106, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.149, compare 1.57, etc.; οἷα φρονῶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 334; δ. περί τινος, ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.72, 73}}: with _accusative_ et _participle_, σκευή τε γάρ σε καὶ τὸ δύστηνον κάρα δηλοῦτον.. ὄνθ᾽ ὃς εἶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 555}}; ὥς σε δηλώσω κακόν [ὄντα{{Refs|] [prev. work] 783, compare “Ant.” 471: with}}_participle_ _nominative_, referring to the subject, δηλώσω πατρὶ μὴ ἄσπλαγχνος γεγώς I **will show** my father that I am no dastard, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 472}}; δηλοῖς.. τι καλχαίνουσ᾽ ἔπος thou **showest** that thou art pondering.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 20; δηλοῖς ὥς τι σημανῶν [prev. work] 242}}; δηλώσω οὐ παραγενόμενος I **will show** that I was not present, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.8; δηλώσει μείζων γεγενημένος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21; also Λιβύη δηλοῖ ἑωυτὴν ἐοῦσα περίρρυτος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42; ἑαυτὸν ἐδήλωσεν ὅστις ἦν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.48.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to be clear** or **plain,** δηλοῖ ὅτι οὐκ Ὁμήρου τὰ Κύπρια ἔπεά ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.117; δηλοῖ δὲ ταῦτα.. ὅτι οὕτως ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 483d; δηλώσει ἡ ἔχθρα ὅταν πρῶτον.. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.12}}; **to be significant, possess a meaning,** with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 434c.}}
**II.2** _impersonal_ δηλοῖ, ={δῆλόν ἐστι}, δηλοῖ μοι ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.68, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296a20; δηλώσει 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 497c; ἐδήλωσε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.32, compare “Cyr.” 7.1.30.}}

1215:
** δημηγορ-έω**,
**practise speaking in the assembly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 956, etc.; πρὸ τοῦ πολιτεύεσθαι καὶ δ. ἐμέ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.60; δ. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.45; δ. πρός τινας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 817c; ἐν τοῖς ὄχλοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 83: with _accusative_ _cognate_, δ. καὶ συνηγορεῖν λόγους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.15; δ. λόγον παρά τισι [prev. author] 23.110}}:—_passive_, τὰ δεδημηγορημένα **public speeches,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.9.}}
**II** especially **make popular speeches, use clap-trap,** ταῦτα δημηγορεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482c: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 503b, “Tht.” 162d, “R.” 350e; τῶν δημηγοριῶν ὧν δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.202}}; δ. πρὸς χάριν, πρὸς ἡδονήν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 3.3, 4.38, compare 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ μεθόδου δεινότητος” 1.}}

1217:
** δημιουργ-ός**,
_Epic dialect_ **δημιοεργός** (also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.31 codices)}}, ὁ, **one who works for the people, skilled workman, handicraftsman** (opposed to ἰδιώτης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298c, “Prt.” 327c, “Ion” 531c), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.383, 19.135; ἐχάλκευσε ξίφος.. Αιδης δ. ἄγριος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1035}}; of medical **practitioners,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 186d}}; but opposed to scientific physicians (ἀρχιτεκτονικοί), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282a3}}; of sculptors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 529e}}; of confectioners and cooks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.31, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 518.12 (_feminine_), 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 225, Alexandr.Comedy texts 3}}; μέλιτος δ., of the bee, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 8.241a}}; οἱ δ. **the artisan class** at Athens, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 13.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 25}}; opposed to πολιτικοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23e}}; δαμιουργοί, ={πόρναι}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **maker,** ἡ μαντικὴ φιλίας θεῶν καὶ ἀνθρώπων δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188d}}; νόμων, πολιτείας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273b32; λόγων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.215}}; δ. κακῶν **author** of ill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1059.7; πειθοῦς δ. ἡ ῥητορική 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 453a; ἀρετῆς [prev. author] “R.” 500d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1329a21; ἐναργείας 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 215}}; ὄρθρος δημιοεργός morn **that calls man to work,** {{Refs|hymnus ad Mercurium 98.}}
**3** **creator, producer,** νυκτός τε καὶ ἡμέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 40c; οὐρανοῦ [prev. author] “R.” 530a}}; especially in later philosophy, **the Creator** of the visible world, **Demiurge,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus] 21, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 23, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.632, etc.; ὁ νοῦς ἀπεκύησε ἕτερον νοῦν δ. “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.9}}; also name for μονάς, {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 5.24: as}} _adjective_, δ. λόγος **creative** reason, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Syrianus Philosophus et Rhetor “in Metaphysica commentaria - Comm. in” 7.27.}}
**II** in many Greek states, title of **a magistrate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47 (from Mantinea), Epist. Philipp. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.157 (from Peloponnesus), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.5.16 (Achaean League)}}, etc.:—_Doric dialect_ **δαμιωργός**, {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).174 (from Astypalaea)}}; **δαμιουργός**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.679 (from Hermione)}}; **δαμιοργός**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]5(1).1390.116 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; **δαμιεργός**, {{Refs|[prev. work]12(3).168 (from Astypalaea)}}:—_Ionic dialect_ **δημιοργός**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]12(7).241 (from Amorgos), “Michel” 368.1 (from Samos).—In 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275b29 there is a play upon the double meaning.}}
**III** as a priestly title, δ. θεᾶς Ῥώμης {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 937.9 (3rd c.AD).}}

1218:
** δῆμος**,
_Doric dialect_ **δᾶμος** (compare infr. IV), ὁ, **district, country, land,** Βοιωτοὶ μάλα πίονα δ. ἔχοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.710; Λυκίης ἐν πίονι δ. 16.437, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.322, etc.; 'Ιθάκης ἐνὶ δ. 1.103; δήμῳ ἔνι Τρώων 13.266; λαοὶ ἀνὰ δῆμον 16.95: _metaphorically_, δῆμος ὀνείρων}} **the land** of dreams, {{Refs|24.12.}}
**2** the **people, inhabitants** of such a district, πόληΐ τε παντί τε δήμῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.50, compare “hymnus ad Cererem” 271; Βακτρίων ἔρρει πανώλης δ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 732.}}
**II** hence (since the common people lived in the country, the chiefs in the city), **the commons, common people,** δήμου ἀνήρ, opposed to βασιλεύς, ἔξοχος ἀνήρ, etc., {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.198, 188, compare 11.328, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 261, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.66, NT.Act.12.22, etc. (rarely of a single person, δῆμον ἐόντα}} being **a commoner,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.213)}}; opposed to οἱ εὐδαίμονες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196}}; opposed to οἱ παχέες, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.30}}; opposed to οἱ δυνατοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.4; οἱ.. ἐπαναστάντες τοῖς δυνατοῖς καὶ ὄντες δῆμος [prev. author] 8.73}}; = Latin _plebs,_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.88, etc.; τοῦ πολλοῦ δ. εἷς}}**unus de plebe,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 2; τοῦ δ. ὤν [prev. author] “Gall.” 22}}; in an army, **rank and file,** opposed to officers, ὁ δ. τῶν στρατιωτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.14.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, δ. ἰχθύων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 206.7; τυράννων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.15.1; πιθήκων [prev. author] “VA” 3.4}}; ὀρνέων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.30.}}
**III** in a political sense, **the sovereign people, the free citizens,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 199, 1011, etc.; ὁ δ. ὁ Ἀθηναίων “IG” 12.10.37, etc.; προστάτης τοῦ δήμου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.35, etc.; personified, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 42, +others; ἱερεὺς τοῦ Δ. καὶ τῶν Χαρίτων “IG” 22.1028.}}
**III.2** **popular government, democracy,** opposed to ὀλιγαρχίη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82}}; opposed to οἱ τύραννοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.106; πολίτεομα εἶναι ἐν Χίῳ δ. “SIG” 283.4 (4th c.BC)}}; δήμου κατάλυσις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.28, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 8.4; ταῦτα καταλύει δῆμον, οὐ κωμῳδία 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 25.7}}; δ. καταστῆσαι, καταπαύειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107}}: in _plural_, **democracies,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.82, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.15; δ. ὁ ἔσχατος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277b3.}}
**III.3** **the popular assembly,** λέγειν ἐν τῷ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 565b}}; ἡ βουλὴ καὶ ὁ δ., formula in Inscrr., as {{Refs|“IG” 12.39, etc.}}; of the **assembly** of Oxyrhynchus, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 41.19 (3rd-4th c.AD), 1407.19 (3rd c.AD).}}
**IV** **township, commune** (= _Doric dialect_ κώμη _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1448a37; but διελόμενοι τὴν μὲν πόλιν κατὰ κώμας, τὴν δὲ χώραν κατὰ δήμους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.46, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746d, and see below), in Attica, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.69, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 21.5, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.16, “IG” 12.76.9, +others; elsewhere, [prev. work]12(5).594 (from Ceos), “Phib.” 1.28.13 (3rd c.AD), “OGI” 49.14 (from Ptolemais)}}, etc.:—_Doric dialect_ **δᾶμος**, {{Refs|“Michel” 418.34 (from Calymna), “IG” 12(1).58.23 (from Lindos)}}: in indications of origin, Σωφάνης ἐκ δ. Δεκελεῆθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.73; δήμου Ἁλαιεύς 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 211; τῶν δήμων Πιτθεύς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2b; τῶν δ. Θορίκιος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.30, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 21.4; ἐπιγράψαι τοὺς βουλευτὰς πατρόθεν καὶ τοῦ δ. “IG” 22.223 B 4: _metaphorically_, οἱ τῆς θαλάσσης δ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 44.}}
**V** name for a prostitute, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 184.}}
**VI** **faction** in the circus, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Tab.Defix.Aud.” 15.8 (Syria, 3rd c.AD).}}
**VII** ={κατανάγκη}, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.131}}. (Perh. cognate with Sanskrit _dā´ti_ 'reap', δαίομαι, δατέομαι.)

1219:
** δημόσιος**,
_Doric dialect_ **δᾱμ-**, α, ον (ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus (see. below))}}, **belonging to the people** or **state,** κτέανα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 2.8; τὰδ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 554; δ. χρήματα 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 171; πλοῦτος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80}}; χώρα, opposed to ἱερά, ἰδία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1267b34; ἡ δ. τράπεζα “IG” 22.1013}}; τὰ ἱερὰ τὰ δ., opposed to ἰδιωτικά, {{Refs|“SIG” 1015.9 (Halic.)}}; ἀγῶνες, δίκαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1320a12}}; δ. λόγος, = Latin _fiscus_, {{Refs|“BGU” 193.27, “OGI” 1669.21}}; δημόσιον εἶναι, γίγνεσθαι, to be, become **state-property,** be **confiscated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, “IG” 22.1100.40 (2nd c.AD: Hadrianus Imperator Epigrammaticus), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742b, etc.; γῆν δ. ποιεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.14.}}
**b** **used by the public,** βαλανεῖα, λουτρόν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 26.1.12, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.12.4.}}
**2** **common,** δημοσιώτατος τρόπος, τόπος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 162a35, “SE” 165a5; δημόσιος κακίη}}**epidemic,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 19 (“Hermes” 53.67).}}
**II** as _substantive_:
**II.a** δημόσιος (i.e. δοῦλος), ὁ, **any public slave** or **servant,** as, **the public crier,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.121}}; **policeman,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 436}}; **public notary,** ={γραμματεύς}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.129, etc.}}; **public executioner,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.102}}: generally, **public official,** τὸν ἀρχέφοδον καὶ τοὺς ἄλλους δημοσίους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 69.13 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.b** **public victim,** ={φάρμακος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1136, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])}}
**II.c** **harlot, prostitute,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 9 (compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 148).}}
**III** _neuter_, δημόσιον, τό, **the state,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.58; οἱ ἐκ δ.}}**public officials,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 3.3.}}
**III.b** **public building, hall,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.52.}}
**III.c** **treasury,** ={τὸ κοινόν, ἀργύριον ὀφείλοντες τῷ δ}. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.73, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.182, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.2; ὁ ἐκ δ. μισθός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31; ἡ ἐκ τοῦ δ. τροφή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465d; τελεῖν εἰς τὸ δ. “BGU” 1188.12 (Aug.), 1158.18 (1st c.BC).}}
**III.d** **the public prison,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.18.}}
**III.2** τὰ δ. **public archives**, {{Refs|“OGI” 229.108 (from Smyrna).}}
**III.2.b** **public dues, taxes,** in _plural_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.938.11 (3rd c.AD), “BGU” 1018.21 (3rd c.AD).}}
**IV** _feminine_, δαμοσία (i.e. σκηνή), ἡ, **tent of the Spartan kings**: hence οἱ περὶ δαμοσίαν the **king's council,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.8, “Lac.” 13.7.}}
**V** as _adverb_:
**V.1** _dative_ δημοσία, _Ionic dialect_ -ίῃ, **at the public expense,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 396, etc.}}; **by public consent,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.50}}; **on public service,** δ. ἀποδημεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] 45.3}}; δ. κρίνειν try **in the public courts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.105}}; δ. τεθνάναι to die **by the hands of the public executioner,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.81.}}
**V.2** **as a community,** opposed to ἰδίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30b.}}
**V.3** **commonly, popularly,** τὰ δ. νομιζόμενα ἀγαθά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 4.}}
**V.4** regular _adverb_ **-ίως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 151.12}}; **on public business,** καταπλεῦσαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 520.7 (Naxos, 3rd c.BC).}}

1221:
** δήποτε**,
_indefinite_ _adverb_ (better written δή ποτε), _Ionic dialect_ **δήκοτε**, _Doric dialect_ **δήποκα**, **at some time, once upon a time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.162, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1131 (Lyric poetry); αἰεὶ δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.73}}; **at length,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 577.}}
**2** εἰ δή ποτε if **ever,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.40.}}
**3** with _interrogative_, τί δή ποτ; what **in the world?** what or why **now?** καίτοι τί δή ποτ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.35}}; πόσοι δή ποτ᾽ εἰσὶν οἱ..; how many **do you suppose?** {{Refs|[prev. author] 20.21.}}
**4** especially frequently with relatives, ὅτι δή κοτε πράξαντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.134; ὅστις δ. ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 273c}}; ὅτι δή ποτε whatever **it may be,** **'so-and-so',** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.32; ὁπόθεν δ. [prev. author] 35.6: strengthened by οὖν, ὅντινα δή ποτ᾽ οὖν τρόπον [prev. author] 40.8}}; οἷος δή ποτ᾽ οὖν variant in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.10}}; also δή ποτ᾽ οὖν without _relative_, κατὰ πρεσβείαν ἢ κατ᾽ ἄλλην δ. χρείαν {{Refs|“Arch.Papyrus” 6.9 (from Delos).}}

1222:
** δήπου**,
_indefinite_ _adverb_ (better written δή που) **perhaps, it may be,** ᾧ δή που ἀδελφεὸν ἔκτανε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.736}}: in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ usually **doubtless, I presume,** οὐ δήπου τλητόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1064; τῶν Ααΐου δ. τις ὠνομάζετο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1042, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 491, 582, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121, etc.}}; ἴστε γὰρ δή που, μέμνησθε γὰρ δή που, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.25, 19.113, compare 18.249; σχεδὸν ἴσμεν ἅπαντες δή που [prev. author] 3.9; οὐδεὶς ἀγνοεῖ δή που [prev. author] 21.156.}}
**II** as _interrogative_ implying an affirmative answer, τὴν αἰχμάλωτον κάτοισθα δή πο; i.e. **I presume** you know, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 418; ἀνόμοιον δή που 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 159b}}; οὐ δή πο; **surely it is** not **so?** implying a _negative_ answer, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 526, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 73c.}}

1223:
** διά**,
_poetry_ **διαί** (_Aeolic dialect_ ζά, which see), _preposition_ governing _genitive_ and _accusative_ — Rad. sense, through; never _anastrophic_ [Prop. δῐᾰ: but {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} uses ῑ at the beginning of a line, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.357, 4.135, +others}}: also ᾱ, for the sake of meter, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, for which 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} uses **διαί** in Lyric poetry, {{Refs|“Ag.” 448, +others]}}
**A** WITH GEN.
**A.I** of Place or Space:
**A.I.1** of motion **in a line,** from one end to the other, **right through,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} frequently of the effect of weapons, διὰ μὲν ἀσπίδος ἦλθε.. ἔγχος καὶ διὰ θώρηκος.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.357; δουρὶ βάλεν Δάμασον κυνέης διά 12.183; δι᾽ ὤμου.. ἔγχος ἦλθεν 4.481; in Prose, τιτρώσκειν διὰ τοῦ θώρακος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.26; διὰ τοῦ ὀρόφου ἐφαίνετο πῦρ [prev. work] 7.4.16: also of persons, διὰ Σκαιῶν πεδίονδ᾽ ἔχον ὠκέας ἵππους}} **out through** the Scaean gate, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.263}}; δι᾽ ἠέρος αἰθέρ᾽ ἵκανεν **quite through** the lower air even to the ether, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.288, compare 2.458}}; διὰ Τρώων πέτετο **straight through** them,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.755; δι᾽ ὄμματος.. λείβων δάκρυον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1250, etc.}}: also in Compos. with πρό and ἐκ, see at {διαπρό},{διέκ}: in adverbial phrases, διὰ πασῶν (i.e. χορδῶν), see at {διαπασῶν}:from beginning to end,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 275, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 519c, etc.: with _adjectives_ alone, δ. παντός}}**continually,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 862 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; δι᾽ ὀλίγου **for** a short time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; δ. μακροῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 320; ὁ δ. μέσου χρόνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.27.}}
**A.I.2** of the interval which has passed **between** two points of Time, δ. χρόνου πολλοῦ or δ. πολλοῦ χρ. **after** a long time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1045; δ. μακρῶν χρόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 22d: without an}} _adjective_, δ. χρόνου **after** a time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 758, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.28, etc.}}; δι᾽ ἡμερῶν **after** several days, {{NT|NT.Mark.2.1}}; and with _adjectives_ alone, δι᾽ ὀλίγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.14; οὐ δ. μακροῦ [prev. author] 6.15, 91; δ. πολλοῦ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 2, etc.: with Numerals, δι᾽ ἐτέων εἴκοσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.118, compare “OGI” 56.38 (3rd c.BC), etc.: but δ. τῆς ἑβδόμης}} **till** the seventh day, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 21: also distributively, χρόνος δ. χρόνου προὔβαινε}} time **after** time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 285; ἄλλος δι᾽ ἄλλου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1248.}}
**A.I.3** of successive Intervals, δ. τρίτης ἡμέρης every other day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37; δ. τρίτου ἔτεος [prev. work] 4, etc.}}; δ. πεντετηρίδος every four years (with inclusive reckoning), {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.97}}; δι᾽ ἔτους πέμπτου, of the Olympic games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 584 (but δι᾽ ἑνδεκάτου ἔτεος}} **in the course of** the eleventh year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62).}}
**A.III** causal, **through, by,**
**A.III.a** of the Agent, δι᾽ ἀλλέλων or -ου ἐπικηρυκεύεσθαι, ποιεῖσθαι, **by the mouth of..,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.69, 6.4, compare 1.113; δι᾽ ἑρμηνέως λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.17, etc.; τὸ ῥηθὲν ὑπὸ Κυρίου δ. τοῦ προφήτου NT.Matt.1.22; δι᾽ ἑκόντων ἀλλ᾽ οὐ δ. βίας ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 58b}}; πεσόντ᾽ ἀλλοτρίας διαὶ γυναικός **by** her **doing,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 448 (Lyric poetry); ἐκ θεῶν γεγονὼς δ. βασιλέων πεφυκώς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.24}}; δι᾽ ἑαυτοῦ ποιεῖν τι **of oneself, not by another's agency,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.1.4}}, etc.; but also, **by oneself alone, unassisted,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.14, compare 22.38.}}
**A.III.b** of the Instrument or Means, δ. χειρῶν **by** hand (properly **by holding between** the hands), δι᾽ ὁσίων χ. θιγών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 470}}; also δ. χερῶν λαβεῖν, δ. χειρὸς ἔχειν **in** the hand, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 916, 1258 (but τὰ τῶν ξυμμάχων δ. χειρὸς ἔχειν}} to keep a **firm** hand on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13); δ. στέρνων ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 639; ἡ ἀκούουσα πηγὴ δι᾽ ὤτων [prev. author] “OT” 1387; δ. στόματος ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.25; δ. μνήμης ἔχειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 9; αἱ δ. τοῦ σώματος ἡδοναί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.6}}; δ. λόγων συγγίγνεσθαι to hold intercourse **by** word, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272b; δ. λόγου ἀπαγγέλλειν NT.Act.15.27; δι᾽ ἐπιστολῶν NT.2Cor.10.9, “POxy.” 1070.15 (3rd c.AD).}}
**A.III.c** of Manner (where διά with its Noun frequently serves as an _adverb_), δ. μέθης ποιήσασθαι τὴν συνουσίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176e}}; παίω δι᾽ ὀργῆς **through** passion, **in** passion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 807}}; δ. τάχους, ={ταχέως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 822, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.63 (but δ. ταχέων [prev. work] 80, +others)}}; δ. σπουδῆς **in** haste, hastily, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 212}}; δι᾽ αἰδοῦς **with** reverence, respectfully, {{Refs|[prev. work] 441}}; δ. ψευδῶν ἔπη lying words, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 309}}; αἱ δ. καρτερίας ἐπιμέλειαι **long-continued** exertions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.20}}; δι᾽ ἀκριβείας, δ. πάσης ἀκρ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 23d, “Lg.” 876c; δ. σιγῆς [prev. author] “Grg.” 450c; δ. ξυμφορῶν ἡ ξύμβασις ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.10; οὐ δι᾽ αἰνιγμάτων, ἀλλ᾽ ἐναργῶς γέγραπται 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.121; δι᾽ αἵματος, οὐ δ. μέλανος τοὺς νόμους ὁ Δράκων ἔγραψεν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 17: also with _adjectives_, δ. βραχέων, δ. μακρῶν τοὺς λόγους ποιεῖσθαι, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 449b; ἀποκρίνεσθαι δ. βραχυτάτων [prev. passage] d; compare infr. IV.}}
**A.III.2** in later Prose, of Material **out of** which a thing is made, κατασκευάζειν εἴδωλα δι᾽ ἐλέφαντος καὶ χρυσοῦ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.115; θυσίαι δι᾽ ἀλφίτου καὶ σπονδῆς πεποιημέναι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 8; βρώματα δ. μέλιτος καὶ γάλακτος γιγνόμενα 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.646e; οἶνος δ. βουνίου 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.46.}}
**A.IV** διά τινος ἔχειν, εἶναι, γίγνεσθαι, to express conditions or states, ἀγὼν διὰ πάσης ἀγωνίης ἔχων extending **through** every kind of contest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91; δι᾽ ἡσυχίης εἶναι [prev. author] 1.206}}; δι᾽ ὄχλου εἶναι to be trouble**some,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 888; δ. φόβου εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.59; δι᾽ ἀπεχθείας γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.2; ἡ ἐπιμέλεια δ. χάριτος γίγνεται [prev. passage]; δ. μιᾶς γνώμης γίγνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.138.}}
**A.IV.b** with Verbs of motion, δ. μάχης ἐλεύσονται will engage **in** battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9; ἐλθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92}}; δ. παντὸς πολέμου, δ. φιλίας ἰέναι τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.8}}; δ. δίκης ἰέναι τινί go **to** law with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 742, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60; δ. τύχης ἰέναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 773; δι᾽ ὀργῆς ἥκειν [prev. author] “OC” 905}}; ἐμαυτῷ δ. λόγων ἀφικόμην I held converse with myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 872}}; δ. λόγων, δ. γλώσσης ἰέναι come **to** open speech, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 916, “Supp.” 112}}; δ. φιλημάτων ἰέναι come **to** kissing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Andr.” 416; δ. δικαιοσύνης ἰέναι καὶ σωφροσύνης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 323a, etc.}}; δ. πυρὸς ἰέναι (see. πῦρ): in _passive_ sense, δι᾽ ἀπεχθείας ἐλθεῖν τινι to be **hated** by.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 121 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**A.IV.c** with _transitive_ Verbs, δι᾽ αἰτίας ἔχειν or ἄγειν τινά hold **in** fault, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 9.32; δι᾽ ὀργῆς ἔχειν τινά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, etc.; δ. φυλακῆς ἔχειν τι [prev. author] 7.8}}; δι᾽ οἴκτου ἔχειν τινά, δι᾽ αἰσχύνης ἔχειν τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 851, “IT” 683; δ. πένθους τὸ γῆρας διάγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.6; δι᾽ οὐδενὸς ποιεῖσθαί τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 584.}}
**B** WITH Acc.
**B.I** of Place, only poetical, in same sense as διά with _genitive_:
**B.I.1** **through,** ἓξ δὲ δ. πτύχας ἦλθε.. χαλκός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.247; ἤϊξε δ. δρυμὰ.. καὶ ὕλην 11.118, compare 23.122, etc.}}; δ. τάφρον ἐλαύνειν **across** it,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|12.62; δ. δώματα ποιπνύοντα 1.600; ἐπὶ χθόνα καὶ δ. πόντον βέβακεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).41; φεύγειν δ. κῦμ᾽ ἅλιον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 14 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**B.I.2** **through, among, in,** οἴκεον δι᾽ ἄκριας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.400; ἄραβος δὲ δ. στόμα γίγνετ᾽ ὀδόντων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.375 (but μῦθον, ὃν.. δ. στόμα.. ἄγοιτο}} **through** his mouth,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.91; so δ. στόμα ὄσσαν ἱεῖσαι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 65; ἀεὶ γὰρ ἡ γυνή σ᾽ ἔχει δ. στόμα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 855); δ. κρατερὰς ὑσμίνας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 631; νόμοι δι᾽ αἰθέρα τεκνωθέντες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 867 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II** of Time, also poetical, δ. νύκτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.57, etc.}}; δ. γλυκὺν ὕπνον **during** sweet sleep, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 4.91.}}
**B.III** causal:
**B.III.1** of persons, **thanks to, by aid of,** νικῆσαι δ... Ἀθήνην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.520, compare 13.121; δ. δμῳὰς.. εἷλον 19.154}}; δ. σε **by** thy **fault** or **service,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1129, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 145, compare 160, 170}}: in Prose, **by reason of, on account of,** δ᾽ ἡμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.41, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.249; οὐ δι᾽ ἐμαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.144}}; so εἰ μὴ διά τινα if it had not been **for..,** εἰ μὴ δι᾽ ἄνδρας ἀγαθούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.60; Μιλτιάδην εἰς τὸ βάραθρον ἐμβαλεῖν ἐψηφίσαντο, καὶ εἰ μὴ δ. τὸν πρύτανιν ἐνέπεσεν ἄν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516e, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.74; εἰ μὴ δ. τὴν ἐκείνου μέλλησιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 558; πλέον᾽ ἔλπομαι λόγον Ὀδυσσέος ἢ πάθαν γενέσθαι δι᾽ Ὅμηρον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.21.}}
**B.III.2** of things, to express the Cause, Occasion, or Purpose, δι᾽ ἐμὴν ἰότητα **because of** my will, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.41; Διὸς μεγάλου δ. βουλάς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.82}}; δι᾽ ἀφραδίας **for, through** want of thought,{{Refs|19.523; δι᾽ ἀτασθαλίας 23.67}}; δι᾽ ἔνδειαν **by reason of** poverty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.6}}; δ. καῦμα, δ. χειμῶνα,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.7.6; δι᾽ ἄγνοιαν καὶ ἀμαθίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 360b, etc.}}: frequently also with _neuter_ _adjectives_, δ. τ; **wherefore?;** δ. τοῦτο, δ. ταῦτα **on** this **account;** δι᾽ ὅ, δι᾽ ἅ **on** which **account;** δ. πολλά **for** many **reasons,** etc.
**B.III.3** ={ἕνεκα}, to express Purpose, δἰ ἀχθηδόνα **for the sake of** vexing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.40, compare 5.53}}; δ. τὴν τούτου σαφήνειαν **with a view to** clearing this up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 524c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1172b21}}; αὐτή δι᾽ αὑτήν **for** its own **sake,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 367b, etc.}}
**C** WITHOUT CASE as _adverb_ **throughout,** δ. πρό (see. supr. A.I.I); δ. δ᾽ ἀμπερές {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.377.}}
**D** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**D.I** **through, right through,** of Space, διαβαίνω, διέχω, διιππεύω.
**D.II** **in different directions,** as in διαπέμπω, διαφορέ; of separation, **asunder,** διαιρέω, διαλύ; of difference or disagreement, **at variance,** διαφωνέω, διαφέρ; or simply mutual relation, **one with another,** διαγωνίζομαι, διάδω, διαθέω, διαπίνω, διαφιλοτιμέομαι.
**D.III** pre-eminence, διαπρέπω, διαφέρω.
**D.IV** completion, **to the end, utterly,** διεργάζομαι, διαμάχομαι, διαπράττω, διαφθείρω: of Time, διαβιόω.
**D.V** to add strength, **thoroughly, out and out,** διαγαληνίζω, etc.; compare ζά.
**D.VI** of mixture, **between, partly,** especially in _adjective_, as διάλευκος, διάχρυσος, διάχλωρος, etc.
**D.VII** of leaving an **interval** or **breach,** διαλείπω, διαναπαύω. (Cogn. with δύο, δίς.)

1224:
** διαβαίνω**,
_future_ -βήσομαι: _aorist_ -έβην, _Aeolic dialect_ _participle_ ζάβαις {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.3:}}
**I** _intransitive_, **stride, walk** or **stand with legs apart,** εὖ διαβάς, of a man **planting himself firmly** for fighting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.458, 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.21; ὡδὶ διαβάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 688; τοσόνδε βῆμα διαβεβηκότος [prev. author] “Eq.” 77}}; opposed to συμβεβηκώς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.14; πόδας μὴ -βεβῶτας 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 43, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.76; κολοσσοὶ -βεβηκότες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.779f}}; simply, **spacious,** δόμοι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 15: _metaphorically_, μεγάλα δ. ἐπί τινα}} **to go with** huge **strides** against.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 32}}; ὀνόματα -βεβηκότα εἰς πλάτος **great straddling** words, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22}}; [ποὺς] -βεβηκώς **with a mighty stride,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 17: with _accusative_ _cognate_, αἱ ἁρμονίαι διαβεβήκασι εὐμεγέθεις διαβάσεις [prev. work] 20}}; also ἐξερείσματα χρόνων πρὸς ἑδραῖον -βεβηκότα μέγεθος {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.4.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **step across, pass over,** τάφρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.50; πόρον᾽ Ωκεανοῖο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 292, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 865 (Lyric poetry); Ἀχέροντα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus [same place]; ποταμόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75, etc., compare 7.35; also διὰ ποταμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.2.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_ (θάλασσαν or ποταμόν being omitted), **cross over,** Ἤλιδ᾽ ἐς εὐρύχορον διαβήμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.635; [ἐς] τήνδε τὴν ἤπειρον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.118; πλοίῳ [prev. author] 1.186, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.114, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229c, etc.: _metaphorically_, τῷ λόγῳ διέβαινε ἐς Εὐρυβιάδεα}} **he went over** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.62; δ. ἐπὶ τὰ μείζω 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.18.18.}}
**II.2.b** πόθεν.. διαβέβηκε τὸ ἀργύριον from what sources the money **has mounted up,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.829e.}}
**II.3** **bestride,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 5.54 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.4** **decide,** δίκας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 426.7 (Teos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.5** **come home to, affect,** εἴς τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Oen.2, 7th c.AD: Stephanus Philosophus “in Artem Rhetoricam commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 281.5.}}

1225:
** διαβάλλω**,
_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _perfect_ -βέβληκα:— **throw** or **carry over** or **across,** νέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33, 34}}; in wrestling, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 262 codices}}
**2** more frequently _intransitive_, **pass over, cross,** ἐκ.. ἐς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.114; φυγῇ πρὸς Ἄργος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 931; πρὸς τὴν ἤπειρον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.83: with _accusative_ spatii, δ. πόρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 69 (uncertain); γεφύρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 117; τὸν Ἰόνιον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.30; τὸ πέλαγος εἰς Μεσσαπίους 5th-6th c.BC: Demetrius Comicus Veteris Comoediae 1.}}
**3** **put through,** τῆς θύρας δάκτυλον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.118; τύλος διαβεβλημένος διὰ τοῦ ῥυμοῦ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.7 (= 4th c.BC: Aristobulus Historicus “fragment” 4); κρίκων δι᾽ ἀλλήλων διαβεβλημένων 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 30.20; διαβληθέντων τῶν ἀγκώνων διὰ μέσων τῶν τόνων 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 101.12, compare 108.6.}}
**II** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 643 ἅττα διαβάλοι τις αὐτῷ, ταῦτ᾽ ἂν ἥδιστ᾽ ἤσθιεν, for παραβάλοι}} whatever scraps **they threw to** him, with a play on _signification_ see
**III** **set at variance,** ἐμὲ καὶ Ἀγάθωνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222c, 222d, compare “R.” 498c; δ. τινὰς ἀλλήλοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1313b16}}; **set against,** τινὰς πρὸς τὰ πάθη, πρὸς τὴν βρῶσιν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.727d, 730f}}; **bring into discredit,** μή με διαβάλῃς στρατῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 582; δ. [τινὰ] τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566b}}:— _passive_, **to be at variance with,** τινί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 67e}}; **to be filled with suspicion and resentment against** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.35, 6.64, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.81, 83; οὐδὲν ὑπολείπεται ὅτῳ ἄν μοι δικαίως διαβεβλῇσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.24; πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b21, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.19.2; τοὺς -βεβλημένους πρὸς τὴν φιλοσοφίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.175}}; **to be brought into discredit,** ἐς τοὺς ξυμμάχους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.22; διαβεβλημένος}}**discredited,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.27, 8.7.}}
**IV** **put off with evasions,** δ. τινὰ μίαν (i.e. ἡμέραν) ἐκ μιᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5343.41 (2nd c.AD),compare “PFlor.” 36.23 (4th c.AD).}}
**V** **attack** a man's character, **calumniate,** δ. τοὺς Ἀθηναίους πρὸς τὸν Ἀρταφρένεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.96; Πελοποννησίους ἐς τοὺς Ἕλληνας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109}}; διέβαλλον τοὺς Ἴωνας ὡς δι᾽ ἐκείνους ἀπολοίατο αἱ νέες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.90; διαβαλὼν αὐτοὺς ὡς οὐδὲν ἀληθὲς ἐν νῷ ἔχουσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45}}; **accuse, complain of,** without implied malice or falsehood, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 23.4 (2nd c.BC)}}: with _dative_ _of things_, **reproach** a man **with..,** τῇ ἀτυχίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.4}}; δ. τινὰ εἴς or πρός τι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 50, “Macr.” 14}}:—_passive_, διεβλήθη ὡς {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.16.1; ἐπὶ βίῳ μὴ σώφρονι διαβεβλημένος 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.6.6.}}
**V.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **misrepresent,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.225, 28.1, etc.}}: **speak** or **state slanderously,** ὡς οὗτος διέβαλλεν {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.20, compare [prev. work]14}}; τοῦτό μου διαβάλλει {{Refs|[prev. work]28: generally}}**give hostile information,** without any insinuation of falsehood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.4.}}
**V.3** δ. τι εἴς τινα **lay the blame for** a thing on.., {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 22.19.}}
**V.4** **disprove** a scientific or philosophical doctrine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.289}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 5.480, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.930b.}}
**V.5** δ. ἔπος **declare** it **spurious,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Thes.” 34.}}
**VI** **deceive by false accounts, impose upon, mislead,** τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1, 5.50, 8.110, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 435}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.116, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1648 (ubi see Scholia), “Th.” 1214}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.563d.}}
**VII** **divert** from a course of action, πρὸς τὴν κακίαν τινάς {{Refs|[prev. work]809f}}:—_passive_, ψυχὴ -βέβληται πρὸς μάχην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.26.3.}}
**VIII** _middle_, **contract an obligation** (?), {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 9.26.}}
**IX** διαβάλλεσθαι ἀστραγάλοις πρός τινα **throw** against him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.148d, 272f.}}

1227:
** διαβλέπω**,
**stare with eyes wide open,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Insomniis” 462a13}}; δ. εἴς τινα, πρός τινα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 14, 2.548b.}}
**2** **see clearly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 2.13; ἐν τοῖς σκοτεινοῖς 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.252S., compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc. Cond.” 22}}: with _infinitive_, διαβλέψεις ἐκβαλεῖν τὸ κάρφος {{NT|NT.Matt.7.5.}}

1228:
** διάβολ-ος**, ον,
**slanderous, backbiting,** γραῦς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 878, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.24O.}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 45}}; διάβολόν τι, **aliquid invidiae,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.24; τὸ δ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.61d.}}
**II** _substantive_, **slanderer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 297, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 126a31, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.508d}}; **enemy,** {{LXX|LXX.Est.7.4, 8.1}}: hence, = **Sâtân,**{{Refs|[prev. work] 1 “Chr.” 21.1}}; **the Devil,** {{NT|NT.Matt.4.1, etc.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-λως** **injuriously, invidiously,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.15; χρῆσθαί τινι 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 2.}}

1230:
** διαγίγνομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_and later _Attic dialect_ **διαγίνομαι** [γῑ], _future_ -γενήσομαι: _aorist_ διεγενόμην, also διεγενήθην {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 37}}:—**go through, pass,** τοσάδε ἔτη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32e; τὴν νύκτα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.19; δ. ἀπραγμόνως τὴν ἡμέραν 3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.42}}: _absolutely_, **go through life, live,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 45, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.16}}; **survive,** see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.2}}; ἐὰν ἄρα διαγιγνώμεθα if we **live long enough,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.51; ἂν διαγένωμαι 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 66}}; δ. ἀπὸ τῆς τέχνης **to subsist** by it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268a31}}; γενναίως δ. ἔν τινι **behave** nobly in.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.119d: frequently with}}_participle_, διαγενέσθαι ἄρχων **continue in** the government, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.1}}; οὐδὲν ἄλλο ποιῶν διαγεγένηται ἢ διασκοπῶν he **was never anything** but a theorist, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mem.” 4.8.4; δ. κολακεύων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.179.}}
**II** **intervene, elapse,** χρόνου μεταξὺ διαγενομένου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.15; χρόνων διαγενομένων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.9, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 22, etc.}}; οἴδαμεν.. ἤδη τῇ κρίσει ἐκείνῃ διαγεγονότα ἔτη ὀκτώ Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.82, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.162c, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 37, “POxy.” 68.18 (2nd c.AD).}}

