1233:
** διά-γνωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**distinguishing,** τὴν δ. ποιεῖσθαι ὁποῖοι ἐκράτουν ἢ ἐκρατοῦντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.50}}; **means of distinguishing** or **discerning,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 926; καλῶν ἢ μὴ τοιούτων τίς δ.; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.128; δ. φωνῆς καὶ σιγῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 290b27}}; of medical **diagnosis,** δ. ποιεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 10, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.766, etc.}}
**2** **power of discernment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 696.}}
**II** **resolving, deciding,** δ. ποιεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.18; περί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.7; ταχίστην ἔχει δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.34; τοῦ ὃ πρακτέον ἐστίν 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 27}}; δ. τῆς ἀξίας ποιεῖσθαι to **determine** the value, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865c}}; = Latin _cognitio,_ {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.25.21, “BGU” 19i20 (2nd c.AD), 891r24 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐπὶ διαγνώσεων τοῦ Σεβαστοῦ, = Latin _a cognitionibus Augusti,_ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.1072, compare “Ephes.” 3 No.51 (3rd c.AD).}}

1236:
** διάγω** [ᾰ],
**carry over** or **across,** πορθμῆες δ᾽ ἄρα τούς γε διήγαγον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.187, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.78; δ. ἐπὶ σχεδίας ἄρτους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.28.}}
**b** _intransitive_, **cross over,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.2.12.}}
**2** **draw through,** τὴν προβοσκίδα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.968d.}}
**3** Geometry texts, **draw through** or **across, produce** a line, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.21, +others}}
**4** **draw apart,** τὰ ὄμματα {{Refs|“IG” 4.951.121 (Epid.).}}
**II** of Time, **pass, spend,** αἰῶνα {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 20.7}}; βίοτον, βίον, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 711, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1619, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 464; δ. τὸν βίον μαχόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579d; ἡσύχιον βίον δ. ἐν εὐσεβείᾳ NT.1Tim.2.2}}; γῆρας, νύκτα, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.6, “An.” 6.5.1; χρόνον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 10 (but χρόνος διῆγέ με, ={χρόνον διῆγον}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 782)}}]; δ. ἑορτήν **celebrate** it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.363f: hence}}
**II.2** _intransitive_, without βίον, **pass life, live,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 191, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.254, 25.82}}; ={διαιτῶμαι, διατρίβω}, Thom.Mag.p.90,98 R.; δ. ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174b}}; **tarry,** ἐν τῷ δικαστηρίῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Euthphr.” 3e; ἐν προαστείῳ 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.12.5}}:—_middle_, διαγόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344e, etc.; τὰ πρὸς τοὺς θεοὺς εὐσεβῶς δ. “Michel” 352.15 (from Iasus).}}
**II.2.b** **delay,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.3}}: with _accusative_, **spin out, protract,** τοὺς λόγους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.17.}}
**II.2.c** with _accusative_ _person_, **divert, fob off,** ἐλπίδας λέγων διῆγε [τοὺς στρατιώτας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 53, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Phal.” 1.3.}}
**II.2.d** **continue,** δ. σιωπῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.14}}: frequently with _participle_, **continue doing** so and so, δ. λιπαρέοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94}}; δ. μανθάνων, ἐπιμελόμενος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.6, 7.5.85.}}
**II.2.e** with _adverbs_, ἐν τοῖς χαλεπώτατα δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; ἄριστα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.15; εὖ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 625b23; ἀκινδύνως [prev. author] “Pol.” 1295b33}}; also εὐσεβῆ δ. τρόπον περί τινα **conduct oneself** piously, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 457.}}
**III** **cause to continue, keep** in a certain state, πόλιν ὀρθοδίκαιον δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 995 (Lyric poetry); πόλεις ἐν ὁμονοίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.41; ἐν πᾶσι τοῖς κατὰ βίον.. διῆγεν ὑμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.89; τὸ ὑπήκοον ἐν ἡσυχίᾳ δ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.30.}}
**IV** **entertain, feed,** τραγήμασι καὶ λαχάνοις τὸν στρατόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 10.4}}:—_passive_, [λέων] μελιτούτταις διήγετο {{Refs|[prev. author] “VA” 5.42.}}
**V** **manage,** κάλλιστα πάντα δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273c; πανηγυρικώτερον δ. τὰ κατὰ τὴν ἀρχήν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.34.3.}}
**VI** **separate, force apart,** τὰ σκέλεα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 230, LXX.Eze.16.25; τοὺς ὀδόντας 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.6.}}
**VI.2** **divert,** τινὰ ἀπό τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Her.Prooem.” 3}}; simply, **divert,** τὰς βασιλείους φροντίδας {{Refs|[prev. author] “VS” 1.8.2.}}

1237:
** διαδέχομαι**,
_future_ -ξομαι, **receive one from another,** δ. τὸν λόγον **take up** the word, i.e. **speak next,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 576b; λόγον παρά τινος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 8.14: _absolutely_, διαδεξάμενοι ἔλεγον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.142; ἀποκρινόμενοι διαδέχεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 900c}}; δ. νόμους παρὰ τῶν θεῶν, τέχνην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.3, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.6}}; τὴν διατριβήν, leadership of a school of philosophy, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.58 M.}}
**2** δ. βασιλείαν **succeed to** the kingdom, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.4.7; ἀρχὴν παρά τινος [prev. author] 9.28.8}}; τὴν ναῦν δ. τινί, of a trierarch {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (διαδοχή 1), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.38; πλοῦτον παρά τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 11.3.}}
**II** διαδέχεσθαί τινι **succeed** one, **take** his **place, relieve** him on guard, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 758b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.18: later, δ. τινά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299b4, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.3.6}}; δ. τὰ κατὰ τὴν στρατηγίαν **act for** the στρατηγός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 18.3 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; τοὺς προφήτας στολισταὶ δ. **represent,** {{Refs|PGnom. 193.}}
**II.b** **appoint a successor to,** τινά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.231 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator:}} —_passive_, διεδέχθη τῆς στρατηγίας **was relieved of** his command, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] p.243 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **relieve one another,** τοῖς ἵπποις **with fresh** horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.2}} (wrongly explained as **closing in from both sides** by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 93)}}; **succeed,** οἱ διαδεχόμενοι στρατηγοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.62, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1293a29}}; οἱ διαδεξάμενοι **the successors** (of Alexander), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.34.11; οἱ τὰ Πύρρου δ. 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰλλυρική” 7}}: _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, νὺξ εἰσάγει καὶ νὺξ ἀπωθεῖ διαδεδεγμένη **in turns, by turns,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 30; διαδεξάμενοι NT.Act.7.45; οἱ διαδεχόμενοι καιροί 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.8.3.}}
**III** **supersede,** τὸν ὕπατον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 24.1.}}

1238:
** διάδημα**, ατος, τό,
(διαδέω) **band** or **fillet**: especially **band round the** τιάρα worn by the Persian king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.13, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.488d; by Alexander, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.22.2}}; by his successors, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 248.17(Pergam., Antiochus IV), 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.3.3}}; by kings generally, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.753d, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.54; δ. τῆς Ἀσίας LXX.1Mac.13.32.}}
**II** Ὀσίριδος δ., ={ἅλιμος}, Ps.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.91.}}

1239:
** διαδίδωμι**,
_future_ **-δώσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328a}}:—**pass on, hand over,** ἐμοὶ τοῦτον διέδωκαν (i.e. Μοῖσαι) ἀθάνατον πόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 9 “fragment” 16.16; λαμπάδια ἔχοντες διαδώσουσιν ἀλλήλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus [same place]}}; **propound** for consideration, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus}} **in Hp.Fract.**{{Refs|12.277}} C.; **spread abroad,** λόγον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 19:}} — _passive_, λόγος διεδόθη variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.10, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 8; διαδοθέντος τοῦ λόγου 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.7, compare 9.74}}; παρὰ τῶν ἀρχαιων δ. **to be handed down** by tradition, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 270b17; ἐν παροιμίᾳ διαδοθῆναι 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.4.}}
**2** **distribute,** τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.18, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.38, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.14}}:—_passive_, τῇ σάλπιγγι σιωπῆς εἰς ἅπαντας διαδοθείσης {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 10}}; τὸ διαδιδόμενον εἰς τὰς φλέβας, of food, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 678a18}}; of the bowels, **secrete,** τῆς κοιλίης ὑδατόχολα πολλὰ διαδιδούσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 67.}}
**3** δ. κόρας **cast** one's eyes **around,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1371, conjecture in “Or.” 1267.}}
**4** διαδοῦναι δίκας **give** satisfaction to injured party, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** _intransitive_, **spread about,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 495b8.}}
**II.2** **remit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.)” 5.}}

1240:
** διάδοχ-ος**, ον,
**succeeding** a person **in** a thing:
**1** with _dative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, δ. Μεγαβάζῳ τῆς στρατηγίης his **successor in** the command, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.26, compare 1.162, etc.}}; θνητοῖς.. διάδοχοι μοχθημάτων **succeeding** them **in,** i.e. **relieving** them **from,** toils, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 464, compare 1027; σοι τῶνδε διάδοχος δόμων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 655.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_ only, δ. τῆς Ἀστυόχου ναυαρχίας **succeeding to** his command, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.85; δ. τῆς κληρονομίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.43; τῆς φιλοσοφίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 217.}}
**3** with _genitive_ _person_ only, φέγγος ὕπνου δ. sleep's **successor** light, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 867.}}
**4** with _dative_ _person_ only, δ. Κλεάνδρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.5: with _dative_ _of things_, ἔργοισι δ᾽ ἔργα διάδοχα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 743; κακὸν κακῷ δ. [prev. work] 803}}; quasiact., λύπη.. δ. κακῶν κακοῖς **bringing a succession** of evils **after** evils, {{Refs|“Hec.” 588}}; ἀγὼν.. γόων γόοις (γόων bis codices) δ. {{Refs|“Supp.” 72 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5** _absolutely_, διάδοχοι ἐφοίτων they went to work **in relays** or **gangs,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.22, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.110}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, **in turn,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1200 (Lyric poetry).}}
**6** as _substantive_, οἱ Δ. the {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Successors” of Alexander, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.42.}}
**6.b** the lowest grade of court officials at Alexandria, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 100.4, “PAmh.” 2.36.5, “PRyl.” 67.2 (both 2nd c.BC).}}
**6.c** **substitute, deputy**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 852.4 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 54.7 (3rd c.AD).}}
**6.d** **head of a school of philosophers,** τῆς σχολῆς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ind.Sto.” 53; δ. Στωικός “IG” 3.661, compare 22.1009 (Epist. Plotinae).}}
**6.e** a kind of **gem,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 37.157.}}

1241:
** δια-ζώννῡμι**
or **διαζωννύω**, _future_ -ζώσω: _perfect_ _passive_ διέζωμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 2.736 B 19, [prev. work]11(2).161.35 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}:— **gird round, encircle, embrace,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.715: _metaphorically_, τὸν ὅλον ἄνθρωπον διέζωσεν [ἡ ψυχή] 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 39}}:—_middle_, **undergird one's** ship, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.91}}; but usually **gird oneself with,** διαζωσάμενοι τὸ τριβώνιον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 3:}} —_passive_, διαζώννυσθαι ἐσθῆτα, ἀκινάκην, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Somn.” 6, “Anach.” 6: _absolutely_, διεζωσμένοι}} **wearing the** διάζωμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6 codices (-ζωμένοι 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus): _metaphorically_, ἀρχὴν διεζωσμένος}} **invested with** office, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.9.3.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **engirdle, encompass,** of fire, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 31}}; τὸν αὐχένα (i.e. the Chersonese) δ. ἐρύμασι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Per.” 19; νήσους [prev. author] “Them.” 12}}:—_passive_, [ἡ Ἀττικὴ] μέση διέζωσται ὄρεσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.25; ῥάχει διεζῶσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.69.1}}; also **pass like a girdle,** διὰ τῶν τροπικῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392a12.}}

1242:
** διαθήκη**, ἡ,
(διατίθημι) **disposition** of property by will, **testament,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 584, 589, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.13, etc.}}; κατὰ διαθήκην by **will**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 753.8 (from Cilicia), “Test.Epict.” 4.8, “BGU” 1113.5 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.: in _plural_, διαθήκας διαθέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.39; θέσθαι “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2690 (from Iasus).}}
**II** αἱ ἀπόρρητοι δ. mystic **deposits** on which the common weal depended, probably oracles (compare διαθέτης), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.9 codices}}
**II.2** name of an eyesalve, because the recipe was **deposited** in a temple, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.118.}}
**III** **compact, covenant,** ἢν μὴ διαθῶνται διαθήκην ἐμοί {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 440; frequently in LXX, LXX.Gen.6.18, +others}}; καινή, παλαιὰ δ., {{NT|NT.Luke.22.20, NT.2Cor.3.14}}; **disposition** (with allusion to {{NT|1), NT.Gal.3.15, cf. NT.Heb.9.15.}}
**IV** ={διάθεσις}{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|11, σώματος δ. 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 9.}}

1243:
** διαίρ-εσις**, εως, ἡ,
**divisibility,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1016b4, +others}}
**2** in Medicine texts, **dissection,** ζῴων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.664.}}
**2.b** **venesection,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.9.2.}}
**2.c** **surgical operation,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.56O.}}
**2.d** _plural_, **wounds,** {{Refs|Diod.Rh. p.53H.}}
**II** **dividing, distribution,** of money, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144}}; of spoil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.55}}; ἐν διαιρέσει [ψήφων] **in the reckoning** of the votes **on either side,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 749; ἀντίγραφον διαιρέσεως “BGU” 1013.1 (1st c.AD).}}
**III** **distinction,** ἀγνωσίας τε καὶ γνώσεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 267b}}; [τῆς δημοκρατίας καὶ τῆς ὀλιγαρχίας] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1294a34.}}
**IV** in Logic, **division** of genus into species, τῶν γενῶν κατ᾽ εἴδη δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 267d; ἡ διὰ τῶν γενῶν δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 46a31}}; opposed to συναγωγαί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 266b.}}
**IV.b** **separation** of subject and predicate, περὶ σύνθεσιν καὶ δ. ἐστι τὸ ψεῦδος καὶ τὸ ἀληθές {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 16a12.}}
**IV.2** ὁ παρὰ τὴν δ. [λόγος] the fallacy of **division** (compare σύνθεσις), {{Refs|[prev. author] “SE” 177a33.}}
**V** Rhet., **division** or **distribution** into heads, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “προγυμνάσματα” 7, “Stat.” 1, 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 2 at Title Page, etc.}}
**VI** Grammars, **resolution** of a diphthong into two syllables, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 87.2, +others}}; of one word into two, κατὰ διαίρεσιν ἀναγνωστέον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.492a, compare 1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus “περὶ τρόπων” 1.8.}}
**VII** in Metric, **division of a line at the close of a foot, diaeresis,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.24.}}
**VIII** in Mathematics texts, δ. λόγου **transformation** of a ratio **dividendo,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 5 “Def.” 15; κατὰ διαίρεσιν 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.6.}}
**IX** **division** of troops, of the Roman **cohors,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.14.9.}}

1244:
** διαιρ-έω**,
**take apart, cleave in twain, divide,** διὰ δ᾽ ἀμφοτέρους ἕλε κύκλους ἀσπίδος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.280; παῖδα κατὰ μέλεα διελών 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119}}; δ. λαγόν **cut** it **open,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 123}}; δ. πυλίδα **break** it **open,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.110, 6.51; δ. τὴν ὀροφήν}}**tear away, pull down,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.48; τοὺς σταυρούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.21}}; δ. τοῦ τείχους **take down** part of the wall, **make a breach** in it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75}}; τὸ διῃρημένον **the breach,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 76, 5.3}}; διῃρημένοι τὸ ὑπόζωμα, of insects, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 556a18; διαιρουμένος τὴν καρδίαν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 1.}}
**II** **divide,** δύο μοίρας Λυδῶν the Lydians **into** two parts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94, compare 4.148; δύο μερίδας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.12; δ. τριχῇ ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 253c; δ. εἰς τὸ ἐλάχιστον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 440b5; εἰς ὁμοιομερῆ [prev. author] “HA” 486a5 (_passive_)}}:—_middle_, **divide for themselves,** κατ᾽ ὀλίγας ναῦς διελόμενοι **distributing** their ships in small divisions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11}}; τοῖς δικάζουσι δ. τὰ ὦτα **lending** an ear **to both** parties, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 52.4}}; **divide among themselves,** τιμάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 112; τὴν ληΐην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.85; κατὰ πόλεις τὸ ἔργον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.19; τἀδικήματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.38: _absolutely_, δ. κατὰ πόλεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.114:}} —_passive_, διῃρημένοι κατ᾽ ἀναπαύλας **divided** into relays, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.75}}; διαιρήσομαι as _future_ _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 261c; διῄρητο τὰ τῶν Ἑλλήνων εἰς δύο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.51.}}
**II.2** **break up,** opposed to συντιθέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78c, etc.}}
**II.3** **dispense,** φάρμακον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.73b.}}
**III** **distinguish,** τυραννίδος εἴδη δύο διείλομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295a8, etc.; δ. πότερα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.26: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 742}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 182c; δ. τοὺς ἀμείνους καὶ τοὺς χείρονας [prev. author] “Lg.” 950c; δ. περί τινος [prev. author] “Chrm.” 163d.}}
**III.2** **determine, decide,** διαφορὰς διαιρέοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23; δίκας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 472; τοῦτο πρᾶγμα [prev. work] 488}}; ψήφῳ δ. τοῦδε πράγματος πέρι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 630; τὰ ἀμφίλογα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 571a, “Prt.” 314b, +others; κλήρῳ δ. τὸν νικῶντα [prev. author] “Lg.” 946b; δ. περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 239b13, etc.}}; διαιρείσθω πόσα εἴδη, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1300b18, etc.: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1100; also δ. εἴτε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 206 codices}}
**III.3** **define expressly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.γ and 103}}:—_middle_, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.47.}}
**III.4** _middle_, **interpret,** τέρας, δημεῖον, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.60, 9.6.}}
**IV** in Logic, **divide,** δ. κατ᾽ εἴδη τὰ ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 273e}}; **divide** a genus into its species, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 96b15, +others}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 642b5.}}
**V** in Mathematics texts, **divide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 895e (_passive_)}}; διελόντι, **dividendo,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.6, +others}}
**VI** **divide words, punctuate** in reading, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.17, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1401a24 (_passive_)}}; Grammars, **resolve** a diphthong or contracted form, διῃρῆσθαι Ἰακῶς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.17, compare 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 5, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Philet.” p.456P. (_passive_).}}
**VII** **allocate** revenues, {{Refs|“OGI” 573.24 (from Cilicia).}}

1245:
** διακᾰθ-αίρω**,
_aorist_ _participle_ **-άρας**{{Refs|“IG” 11(2).287 A 79}}:—**purge thoroughly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 847, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399e, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.6.7}}; κρουνούς {{NT+4th c.AD+|“IG” [same place]; ἅλωνα NT.Luke.3.17: _metaphorically_, [φιλοσοφία] τέχνας δ. 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 16; τινὰ τοῦ αἰσχροῦ ὀνόματος 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 1.4}}:—_middle_, of **one's own** stock, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 735c.}}
**II** **prune,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.7.2 (_passive_)}}; δένδρα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.207.}}

1247:
** διᾱκον-έω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **διηκ-**, _imperfect_ ἐδιακόνουν {{NT+7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 406 (uncertain), 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus Comedy texts 13, 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Comicus 36; later διηκόνουν NT.Matt.4.11}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 521a}}: _aorist_ διηκόνησα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.198 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; _infinitive_ **-ῆσαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.16}}: _perfect_ δεδιακόνηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Archedicus Comicus 3.8}}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ διηκονούμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 35}}: _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs|[prev. author] “DDeor.” 4.4}}: _aorist_ διηκονησάμην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tyr.” 22}}:—_passive_, _future_ δεδιακονήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.8.7}}: _aorist_ ἐδιακονήθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.2:}}_perfect_ δεδιακόνημαι, see below 11: (διάκονος):—**minister, do service,** _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 396, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1323, “POxy.” 275.10 (1st c.AD)}}: with _dative_ _person_, **serve** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.69, etc.; δ. διακονικὰ ἔργα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1333a8}}; δ. ὑποθήκαις τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.17}}; δ. παρὰ τῷ δεσπότῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 2}}; δ. πρὸς ὠνήν τε καὶ πρᾶσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371d}}:—_middle_, **minister to one's own needs, serve oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 287; αὑτῷ διακονεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1017}}; διακονοῦντες καὶ διακονούμενοι ἑαυτοῖς **acting as servants** and **serving themselves,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 763a}}: also simply like _active_, οἶνον ἡμῖν χρυσίῳ διακονούμενοι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 53, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 53.9}}:—_passive_, **to be served,** οὐκ ἦλθε διακονηθῆναι ἀλλὰ διακονῆσαι {{NT|NT.Matt.20.28.}}
**2** **to be a deacon,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.3.10, 13.}}
**II** c._accusative_ _of things_, **render a service,** τινὶ ὅ τι ἂν δεηθῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 290a; δ. γάμους 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26.19}}:—_passive_, **to be supplied,** τῇ πόλει ἐδιακονήθησαν [αἱ πράξεις] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.2; τῶν καλῶς δεδιακονημένων [prev. author] 51.7: with _dative_ _instrumental_, ἐκπώμασι διακονείσθωσαν “OGI” 383.159 (1st c.BC).}}

1248:
** διᾱκον-ία**, ἡ,
**service,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.133 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371c, etc.; οὔκ ἐστι ταῦτ᾽ ἀρχή, ἀλλ᾽ ἐπιμέλειά τις καὶ δ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.13.}}
**2** **attendance on a duty, ministration,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.206}}; ἡ δ. ἡ καθημερινή, of **ministering** to external wants, {{NT|NT.Act.6.1; but also ἡ δ. τοῦ λόγου [prev. work] 4, compare 1.17, etc.}}
**II** **body of servants** or **attendants,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.25.21.}}
**III** **instruments of service,** ={διακόνημα}{{Refs 4th c.BC+|11, 4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.208b.}}

1249:
** διάκονος** [ᾱ],
_Ionic dialect_ **διήκονος**, ὁ, later **διάκων** (which see):— **servant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71, 72, “PFlor.” 121.3 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; **messenger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 942, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 497; ὄρνιθα καὶ κήρυκα καὶ δ. [prev. author] “fragment” 133:—as _feminine_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1116, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.197.}}
**2** **attendant** or **official** in a temple or religious guild, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Magn.” 109,217, “IG” 9(1).486 (Acarnania, 2nd-1st c.BC), 4.774.12 (Troezen, 3rd c.BC): _feminine_, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3037 (Metropolis in Lydia)}}:—especially in the Christian church, **deacon,** {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.1Tim.3.8, etc., “POxy.” 1162.3 (4th c.AD)}}: _feminine_, **deaconess,** {{NT|NT.Rom.16.1.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **servile, menial,** ἐπιστήμη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 290c}}: irregular _comparative_ διᾱκονέστερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 159 Ahr. (Cf. ἐγ-κονέω, ἀ-κονιτί.)}}

1252:
** διακρίνω** [ρῑ],
_future_ -κρῐνῶ, _Epic dialect_ and Delph. **-κρῐνέω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.387, “SIG” 614.8 (2nd c.BC)}}:—**separate one from another,** ὥς τ᾽ αἰπόλια.. αἰπόλοι ἄνδρες ῥεῖα διακρίνωσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.475, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.114}}; **part** combatants, εἰς ὅ κε δαίμων ἄμμε διακρίνη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.292, etc.; εἰ μὴ νὺξ.. διακρινέει μένος ἀνδρῶν 2.387; δ. φιλέοντε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.179; κρόκην καὶ στήμονας συγκεχυμένους δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 388b}}:—_passive_, **to be parted,** of hair, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 15: more frequently of combatants, διακρινθήμεναι (_Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ _aorist 1_ _passive_) ἤδη Ἀργείους καὶ Τρῶας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.98, compare 102, 7.306}}, etc.: also in _future_ _middle_, διακρινέεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.149, 20.180; διακριθέντες ἐκ τῆς ναυμαχίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.18; διακριθῆναι ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.105, compare 3.9}}; διακρίνεσθαι πρός.. **part and join different** parties, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.18.}}
**b** _passive_, **to be divorced,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium 2.46.}}
**2** in Philosophy, **separate, decompose into elemental parts,** opposed to συγκρίνω, chiefly in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 12, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 985a28, [5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 245, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 71b, “Prm.” 157a, etc.}}
**3** ἄστρων διακρίνει φάη σελάνα probably **sets apart, removes,** i.e. **outshines,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.28.}}
**II** **distinguish,** καί κ᾽ ἀλαὸς διακρίνειε τὸ σῆμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.195}}; οὐδένα δ. without **distinction** of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.39; οὐχὶ δ. τὴν πενιχρὰν ἢ πλουσίαν 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 2.8}}: _perfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, διακεκρίμεθα τάς τε καθαρὰς ἡδονὰς καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 52c:}}_pluperfect_ in _passive_ sense, διεκέκριτο οὐδέν no **distinction was made,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49; διακεκριμέναι}}**distinct, varied,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 24.}}
**III** **decide,** of judges, ὀρθᾷ δ. φρενί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.24; δ. δίκας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.100; διὰ δὲ κρίνουσι θέμιστας 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.46}}; also, **determine** a fever, **mark** its **crisis,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 137}}; ἡ νοῦσος μάλιστα διακρίνει ἐν οὐδενί **has** usually no **crisis** in any patient, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Morb.” 2.71; δ. αἵρεσιν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11; δ. εἰ.. [prev. author] 7.54; δ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 719:}} —_middle_, νεῖκος δ. **get** it **decided,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 35; τὸ ζητούμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 46b}}; **decide among yourselves,** ταῦτα.. ὅπως ποτ᾽ ἔχει δ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.28}}:—_passive_, **bring an issue to decision,** ἐπέεσσί γε νηπυτίοισι ὧδε διακρινθέντε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.212}}; αἴ τινι τᾶν πολίων ᾖ ἀμφίλλογα, διακριθῆμεν Foed._Doric dialect_ cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.79; διακριθεῖμεν περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 7c}}; of combatants, μάχῃ διακριθῆναι πρός τινα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58; πρός τινα ὑπέρ τινος LXX.Joel.3(4).2}}; ὅπλοις ἢ λόγοις διακρίνεσθαι Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.7; διακρίνεσθαι περὶ τῶν ὅλων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.111.2}}; τινί **with** one, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Ep.” “Jud.” 9: _absolutely_, “PMagd.” 1.15 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; also πόλεμος διακριθήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.206}}; of a person, **to be judged,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Callimachus” 18.}}
**IV** **set** [a place] **apart for holy purposes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).46.}}
**V** **interpret** a dream, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.54, 2nd c.AD(?): Juncus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.50.95.}}
**VI** **question,** τοὺς ἰατρούς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.1.148.}}
**VII** **doubt, hesitate, waver,** {{NT|NT.Acts.11.12 (assuming variant): usually in}}_middle_ and _passive_, μηδὲν διακρινόμενος{{Refs|[prev. work]10.20}}; μὴ διακριθῆτε {{NT|NT.Matt.21.21, cf. NT.Rom.4.20.}}

1253:
** διά-κρῐσις**, εως, ἡ,
**separation, dissolution,** opposed to σύγκρισις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 58, 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 243b, +others}}; **segregation,** τῶν ἡμαρτηκότων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.14.8}}; **discrimination,** καλοῦ τε καὶ κακοῦ {{NT|NT.Heb.5.14; πνευμάτων NT.1Cor.12.10}}; **differentiation,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 1.}}
**2** in concrete sense, **resolved form,** ἡ ἀτμὶς ὕδατος δ. ἐστιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 340b3, 341b15.}}
**II** **decision, determination,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 765a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.27, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1169}}; **judicial decision,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PLond. 2.476.9 (1st c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, {{NT|NT.Rom.14.1 (_plural_)}}; **interpretation** of dreams or omens, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.55, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.34.5}}; δ. σημειώσεως medical **diagnosis,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.23}}: but in _plural_, αἱ ἐκ νούσων δ. **determinations, crises,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 3.}}
**II.2** **examination** or **revision of accounts,** δ. πρακτόρων Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Chr.” 41 iii6(3rd c.AD).}}
**III** **decision by battle,** τάξεων πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.28.3}}; **quarrel, dispute,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.29U., 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 109, “Milet.” 3.149.39.}}
**IV** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.1}}, **space between the eyes** in dogs.
**V** **separation** of tumour from bloodvessels, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.2.9.}}
**V.2** **secretion,** οὔρων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.9, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.382.}}
**VI** **a bandage,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(1).777.}}

1255:
** διαλᾰλ-έω**,
**talk with,** τινὶ περί τινος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.85.2, compare “POxy.” 1417.24 (4th c.AD)}}; ἐν ἑαυτοῖς ὑπέρ τινος {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.32.1; κατὰ συμμορίας 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.57; πρὸς ἀλλήλους τί ἂν ποιήσειαν NT.Luke.6.11; αὐτὴν ἐν αὑτῇ δ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.141d.}}
**II** δ. τινί τι **talk over** a thing **with** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 175}}:—_passive_, **to be much talked of,** {{NT|NT.Luke.1.65.}}

1256:
** διαλέγω**,
**pick out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.107, 113, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.9, etc.}}; πτῶμα **glean** fallen olives, {{Refs|“PFay.” 102.20}}; compare διαλέγειν· ἀνακαθαίρειν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; **select, separate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 735b}}; **examine, check** documents, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PFay.” 11.26 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**II** διαλέγων τὴν ὀπήν **picking open** the hole, to escape, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 720}}; compare διαλέξαι· διορύξαι, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**B** as _deponent verb_, διαλέγομαι: _future_ διαλέξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.5 and 112}} also **-λεχθήσομαι** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.34, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.252}}; **-λεγήσομαι** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Perg.” 5 (3rd c.BC)}}: _aorist_ διελεξάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 343}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _imperative_ ζάλεξαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.3; also διελέχθην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.51}}, and always in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 22.657, etc.}}: less frequently _aorist 2_ διελέγην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 154a34, 159a5, 2nd c.BC: Scymnus Geographus 7, “IG” 5(1).5.5 (_Laconian dialect_), “GDI” 5163a2 (from Crete), “PPetr.” 3p.130 (3rd c.BC), “IG” 22.1236}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ διέλεγεν {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3656.7 (Cyzic.)}}: _perfect_ διείλεγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 158c, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.81}}: _pluperfect_ διείλεκτο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.119}}, but in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.5}}:—**hold converse with,** with _dative_ _person_, μοι ταῦτα φίλος διελέξατο θυμός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.407, compare 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 80, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.50, 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 425, etc.; πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 272c, etc.}}; δ. τί τινι or πρός τινα, **discuss** a question with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.10.1, 1.6.1}}; δ. ὅρους **talk in** definitions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 92b32; δ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.252; ἀνὴρ ἀνδρὶ δ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.93}}; δ. τινὶ μὴ ποιεῖν **argue with** one against doing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.59; εἰ τουτὶ τὸ ῥῆμα, ἀλλὰ μὴ τουτὶ διελέχθην ἐγώ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.232}}; οἱ νόμοι οὐδὲν τούτῳ δ. **have** nothing **to say** to him, **concern** him not, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 43.59; ὁ νομοθέτης οὔπω τινὶ δ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.17; δ. πρός τι}}**to argue on..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 159a7}}; or **against..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 185a6}}: _absolutely_, **to discourse, reason,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.12; δ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.109, etc., frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Ap.” 33a, +others; γλῶσσα εὔτροχος ἐν τῷ δ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 7}}; **reason, calculate,** ={διαλογίζομαι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Marc.” 18}}:—the _active_ in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 40; οἱ διαλεγόμενοι, of logicians, Polystr. p.6 W., +others}}
**B.2** in Philosophy, **practise dialectic, elicit conclusions by discussion,** οὐκ ἐρίζειν ἀλλὰ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 454a, compare 511c, “Tht.” 167e, etc.}}
**B.3** later, **discourse, lecture,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.21.3.}}
**B.4** **use a dialect** or **language,** κατὰ ταὐτά τισι δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.142; Φοινικιστί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.80.6}}; **write in prose,** opposed to ποιεῖν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 20.}}
**B.4.b** **speak articulately,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535b2.}}
**B.5** in _Attic dialect_, euphemistic for συνουσιάζω, **have intercourse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 890, “Pl.” 1082, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 171, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 20, Hierocl. p.64A.}}
**B.6** **have dealings with**, {{Refs|“OGI” 484.23 (Pergam.).}}

1257:
** διαλείπω**,
_aorist_ διέλῐπον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 496}}: _perfect_ **-λέλοιπα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.5}}:— **leave an interval between,** τὸ ὀλίγιστον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 226b28}}:—_passive_, διελέλειπτο **a gap had been left,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.40, 41; διαλέλειπται μικρὰ χώρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 503a34.}}
**2** **intermit,** τὴν ὀχείαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 757b4: especially of Time, διαλιπὼν ἡμέρας τὰς συγκειμένας, ἐνιαυτόν}} **having left an interval of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.157, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.8}}; ἀκαρῆ διαλιπών **having waited** an instant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 496; χρόνον ὀλίγον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.8; πολὺν χρόνον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299a37}}; later in _genitive_, μιᾶς ἡμέρας δ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.8.9}}; so οὐ πολὺ διαλιπών **after** a short **time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10: _absolutely_, opposed to εὐθύς, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 198, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 32.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **stand at intervals,** δ. δύο πλέθρα ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.38; πίτυες διαλείπουσαι μεγάλαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.6}}; τὸ δέρμα ταύτῃ δ. **is discontinuous** at this point, opposed to συνεχές ἐστι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 518a3}}; τὸ -λεῖπον **an interval** or **gap,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.13: _impersonal_, διαλείπει}} **there are intervals,** of the heavens, opposed to πλήρη ἀστέρων εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 346a36.}}
**II.2** with _participle_, mostly with _negative_, οὐ πώποτε διέλειπον ζητῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 16, etc.}}; οὐδένα διαλέλοιπα χρόνον διαβαλλόμενος I **have** never **ceased** to be slandered, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.5; οὐ διέλιπον.. παραινῶν πείθεσθαι.. “BGU” 747i 7 (2nd c.AD),compare “POxy.” 281.16 (1st c.AD)}}: without a _negative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 13, “DMeretr.” 11.1.}}
**II.3** of Time, διαλιπόντων ἐτῶν τριῶν, διαλιπούσης ἡμέρας, **after an interval** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.112, 3.74; τὸ διαλεῖπον}}**the interval of time,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 228b4.}}
**II.4** in _participle_, **intermittent,** διαλείποντες πνέουσιν οἱ ἄνεμοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mete.” 362a28, compare “GA” 748a19; δ. πυρετός 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.43, “Coac.” 139.}}
**II.5** **die**, {{Refs|“GDI” 1920.9, 2082.5 (Delph.).}}

1258:
** διάλεκτ-ος**, ἡ,
**discourse, conversation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 30; θεοῖς πρὸς ἀνθρώπους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203a}}; **discussion, debate, argument,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 146b}}; opposed to ἔρις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 454a.}}
**2** **common language, talk,** δ. ἡ πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1449a26; ἡ εἰωθυῖα δ. [prev. author] “Rh.” 1404b24.}}
**II** **speech, language,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 685; καινὴν δ. λαλῶν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 171}}; δ. ἀμνίου, opposed to τὰ ἔνδον δράκοντος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 3}}; **articulate speech, language,** opposed to φωνή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 535a28; τοῦ ἀνθρώπου μία φωνή, ἀλλὰ διάλεκτοι πολλαί [prev. author] “Pr.” 895a6}}; but also, **spoken,** opposed to written **language,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 11.}}
**II.2** **the language of a country,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.80.6, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.6, etc.}}: especially **dialect,** as Ionic, Attic, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Diogenes Babylonius Stoicus 3.213, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.59, Hdn.Gr. 2.932}}; also, **local word** or **expression,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 31.}}
**III** **way of speaking, accent,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.55.}}
**III.2** _plural_, **modes of expression,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.24U.}}
**IV** **style,** πανηγυρική, ποιητικὴ δ., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 23, 21}}: especially **poetical diction,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2 “fragment” 33, +others}}
**V** of musical instruments, **quality, 'idiom',** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 420b8.}}

1259:
** διαλλ-άσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **διαλλάττω**, _future_ -ξω: _perfect_ διήλλαχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus (see. below), 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 70.11.}}
**I** _middle_, **interchange,** τὰς τάξεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.47, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).21}}: _absolutely_, **make an exchange,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.32, “Test.Epict.” 2.14.}}
**II** **exchange,** i.e.,
**II.1** **give in exchange,** τί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 14; τί τινι ἀντὶ ἀργυρίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 371d}}; τινὰ ὑπέρ τινος one for another, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.24; τὴν σκευὴν πρὸς τὸν δεσπότην 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 47.10}}; or,
**II.2** **take in exchange,** δ. ἀετοῦ βίον **take** an eagle's life **for** one's own, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620b; ἐσθῆτα τῇ συμφορᾷ πρέπουσαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 19}}; δ. Μακεδονίαν **change** one land **for** another, i.e. **pass through** a land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.3 (also _absolutely_, ἐξ ἄλλης εἰς ἄλλην πόλιν δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 223d)}}:—_middle_, τι ἀντί τινος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.3.}}
**II.3** simply, **change, alter,** κελεύθους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 35.15; τοὺς ναυάρχους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.4; τοὺς λόγους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1434a38.}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, **change, alter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.12; δ. ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων}}**to be discordant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.6}}; διαλλάττοντας **different,** opposed to ὁμοίους, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 3, +others}}
**II.4.b** **depart this life, die,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator “fragment” 33, 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 35.}}
**II.5** **change money,** δ. τὸ δηνάριον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 484.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** especially **change enmity for friendship, reconcile** one to another, τινά τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.95, 6.47, etc.; πόλεις πρὸς ἀλλήλας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.111: most frequently with _accusative_ _plural_ only, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 436, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.39, Test. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.47, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.91}}: rarely with _accusative_ _singular_, **make it up with** one, διαλλάξεις με φιλάσας {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.42}}:—_passive_ with _future_ διαλλαχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1395, etc.; διαλλαγήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 471a}}: _perfect_ διήλλαγμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 885 (Lyric poetry)}}: _aorist_ **-ηλλάχθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 900, -ηλλάγην [prev. work] 1161}}:—**to be reconciled, to be made friends,** A.[same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346b, etc.; τοῖς ἀποστᾶσι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.63; πρός τινα περί τινος [prev. author] 3.33; τῆς πρόσθεν ἔχθρας ἐς φίλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 896, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.26.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, with _dative_ _person_ et _accusative_ _of things_, **differ from** one **in** a thing, εἶδος δ. οὐδὲν τοῖσι ἑτέροισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.70; δ. ταῖς ἡλικίαις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1161a5; κλήσει, οὐ φύσει 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.29; πρός τινα 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 36(48).16: also with _genitive_ _person_, δ. τινός τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.37.11; ἔν τινι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 23: _absolutely_, πολὺ διήλλαχεν 4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 2.10; τὸ διαλλάσσον τῆς γνώμης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.10:}}_perfect_ _participle_ διηλλαχώς **differing,** τῇ ἐγκλίσει AD [prev. cited]
**IV.2** **excel,** πολὺ δ. τῇ ἀρετῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1165b24; τινῶν τῷ μεγέθει 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.35, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 51:}} — so,
**V** _passive_, **to be different,** τοῖς εἴδεσι διηλλαγμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82; πρὸς τὸν καιρόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 19.}}

1260:
** διαλογ-ίζομαι**,
_perfect_ **-λελόγισμαι**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 33.9}}:—**balance accounts,** πρός τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.3; τινί “PSI” 5.510.10 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 241 C 127 (Delph., 4th c.BC).}}
**2** **calculate exactly,** ὁπόσον.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.15, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus [same place]}}; **consider,** ἀεί τι δ. καλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 112, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.90, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 36}}; κενὰ δ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 347; πρὸς ὑμᾶς αὐτούς 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.45}}; **stop to consider,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.98}}; **distinguish between,** τὰ καλὰ καὶ τὰ μή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.18.}}
**II** **debate, argue,** περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.1.}}
**III** **impute,** τί τινι {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.19.19(20).}}
**IV** with _accusative_ loci. **hold a circuit court** (Latin _conventus_) **for** a district, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 74.8 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 484.24 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐν Ἰονλιοπόλει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 903.18 (2nd c.AD).}}

1261:
** διαλογ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**balancing of accounts,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.23, “PRev.Laws” 17.17 (_plural_), “IG” 5(1).1432.6 (from Messene), etc.: hence}}
**II** **calculation, consideration,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 367a; δ. λαβεῖν περὶ σφῶν αὐτῶν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.3.7}}; ὁ δ. οὗτος this **consideration,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.15.}}
**III** **debate, argument, discussion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 138 (_plural_), 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus 37, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.180c.}}
**IV** **circuit court,** τοῦ νομοῦ δ. ποιῆσαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.358.19, compare “BGU” 19i13 (2nd c.AD).}}
**V** **judicial inquiry,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 27.35 (2nd c.BC), “PFay.” 66.2 (2nd c.AD).}}

1262:
** διαλύω**,
_future_ -λύσω, etc., **loose one from another, part asunder,** διαπλέκων καὶ διαλύων twining and **untwining,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67; νὺξ δ. τοὺς ἀγωνιζομένους [prev. author] 8.11}}; δ. τὸν σύλλογον, τὴν συνουσίαν, τὴν πανήγυριν, etc., **break** it **up, dismiss** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.10.δ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 223b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.10, etc.}}; τὴν σκηνὴν εἰς κοίτην δ. **break up** the party and go to bed, {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.3.1; δ. τὴν στρατιάν [prev. work] 6.1.6}}; τὸ ναυτικόν **disband** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93}}:— _middle_, συνουσίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 457d}}:—_passive_, of an army, assembly, etc., **disperse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.128, etc.; ἐκ τοῦ συλλόγου [prev. author] 3.73, compare 8.56}}: in _future_ _middle_, **part** from one's escort, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.12}}; of a man, **die,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.20.}}
**2** **dissolve** into its elements, **break up, destroy,** δ. καὶ ἀπολλύναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 609a s 1.; ἐξ ἑνὸς εἰς πολλὰ δ. [prev. author] “Ti.” 68d}}; **disperse, break up** a herd of sheep, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“BGU” 1012.12 (2nd c.BC)}}; **break up** a ship, παλαιὰν τριήρη δ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 2.804, compare “PSI” 4.382 (3rd c.BC)}}; τρίπους, ὅρμος διαλελυμένος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 2588.169,198 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; τὰς οἰκήσεις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.65.4}}; **dissolve,** κοινόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Test.Epict.” 8.6; σῴζεσθαι καὶ διαλυθεῖσαν οἴχεσθαι πολιτείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 945c}}; of the sun, **thaw** frozen things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.2}}:—_passive_, ἐξ ὧν σύγκειται καὶ εἰς ἃ διαλύεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 325b19, compare “Ph.” 204b33, etc.}}
**3** **break off, put an end to** friendship, ὁμολογίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.175; φιλίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1157b10}}:—_passive_, of married persons, **separate, be divorced**, {{Refs|“SIG” 364.59 (from Ephesus)}}:—_middle_, διαλύσασθαι ξεινίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154}}: _absolutely_, **dissolve friendship,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1162b25}}:—_passive_, αἱ σπονδαὶ διελέλυντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.1.}}
**4** **put an end to** enmity, ἔχθραν, πόλεμον, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.46}}:—_middle_, δ. ἔχθρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.11; διαφοράς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.160; πολέμους [prev. author] 4.172, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.15: in}}_pluperfect_ _passive_ (with _middle_ _signification_), διελέλυσθε τὸν πόλεμον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.27 (variant{διελύεσθε})}}]:—_passive_, τὰς ἔχθρας διαλύεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19: hence}}
**4.b** with _accusative_ _person_, **reconcile,** πρὸς ἔμ᾽ αὑτὸν διαλύειν ἠξίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.122, compare 41.14; δ. τινὰς ἐκ διαφορᾶς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.87.4; οὐ γὰρ ἦν ὁ διαλύσων οὔτε λόγος οὔτε ὅρκος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83}}; especially in legal proceedings, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHamb.” 25.5 (3rd c.BC), etc.:}} —_passive_ and _middle_, with _genitive_ _of things_, διαλύεσθαι νείκους **to be parted from** quarrel, i.e. be reconciled, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1679 (variant{νείκας})}}]; so διαλυθείσης τῆς διαφορᾶς probably in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.110}}: also _absolutely_, **to be reconciled, make up a quarrel,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.25, cf. Test. cited in 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.66, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 12.14; πρός τινας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.24; περί τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.1}}: in _future_ _middle_, ὅπως.. μὴ διαλύσει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.216.}}
**5** generally, **put an end to, do away with,** χρήμασι τὴν διαβολήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.131}}; πάσας αὐτοῦ διαλύσω τὰς ἀπολογίας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|d. 27.58; τὸν ἐχόμενον φόβον δ. τῶν Ἑλλήνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 241b}}:—so in _middle_, ἐγκλήματα δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140}}; δ. περὶ τῶν ἐγκλημάτων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 145; διαβολάς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.37, 15.16; τι τῶν κατηγορημένων [prev. author] 12.218}}; δ. ἃ ἐψηφίσασθε **cancel** your vote, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.15}}; διαλύσασθαι τὰ πρὸς ἀλλήλους **settle** mutual claims, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.40.}}
**6** **solve** a difficulty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 252d; τὴν ἀπορίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1062b31}}:—_middle_, διαλύσεσθαι σόφισμα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.238.}}
**7** δ.τὰς τιμάς **pay the full** value, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.7}}; **pay, discharge,** τὴν δαπάνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.30; χρήματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.12; τὰ συμβόλαια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1276a11; χρέος τινί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.27.4; πάντα διελέλυτο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.2: also with _accusative_ _person_, δ. τὸν ναύκληρον}} **satisfy** him, i.e. **pay** him **off,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.29, compare 34.40, 36.50}}:—_middle_, **order** debts **to be paid,** διαλέλυμαι ταῦτα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.10.3}}; but also, **to have** them **paid to oneself,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 46.6.}}
**II** **relax, weaken,** τὸ σῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.17}}; especially of the result of hunger, διαλύεσθαι τῷ λιμῷ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 11.27 (2nd c.BC), compare 42.9 (also in}}_active_ _intransitive_, ὑπὸ τῆς λιμοῦ δ.{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 122.23 (2nd c.BC))}}; **make supple and pliant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 85}}:—_passive_, δ. καὶ ἀδυνατεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 585a33}}; ἀνάπλους διαλελυμένος a sailing out **in loose order,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.2.6}}; διαλελυμένη λέξις a **lax** style, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 9.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **slacken one's hold, undo,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.32.}}

1263:
** διαμαρτύρομαι** [ῡ],
_aorist 1_ -μαρτῡράμην: _perfect_ -μεμαρτύρημαι [ῠ], _deponent verb_:—_absolutely_, **call gods and men to witness, protest solemnly,** especially in case of falsehood or wrong, βοᾶν καὶ δ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.23,143}}; δ. μή.., with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 33.20}}; δ. ὅπως μή.., with _future_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 42.28}}; δ. τινὶ μὴ ποιεῖν **protest** against his doing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.89}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.5, +others}}; **call to witness,** ὑμῖν τὸν οὐρανόν {{LXX|LXX.Jdt.7.28.}}
**2** generally, **protest, asseverate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101a, etc., “PSI” 4.422 (3rd c.BC)}}: with _accusative_, **bear witness to,** τὸ εὐαγγέλιον {{NT|NT.Act.20.24}}; **testify,** {{LXX|LXX.Deu.32.46, +others}}; τῇ Ἱερουσαλὴμ τὰς ἀνομίας αὐτῆς{{LXX|LXX.Eze.16.2.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **beg earnestly of** one, **conjure** him, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.9; δ. καὶ παρακαλεῖν NT.Act.2.40}}; δ. τινὰ ἵνα.. {{NT|NT.1Tim.5.21.}}

1264:
** διαμᾰχ-ομαι**,
_future_ -μαχέσομαι (variant for{-μαχεσώμεθα} _aorist_ _subjunctive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.48}}:—**fight, contend,** σιδήρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 678; εἷς πρὸς ἕνα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 833e}}; opposed to λανθάνειν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 345a}}; πρός τι variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.18; περὶ τῆς χώρης τοῖς ἐπιοῦσιν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.11; περὶ τοῦ πρότερος εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 339, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 86c; περὶ τούτου ὡς οὐκ.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.1; ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 207b}}; δ. μὴ μεταγνῶναι ὑμᾶς I **resist to the uttermost** your change of opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40; δ. τὸ μὴ θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 694.}}
**2** **exert oneself, strive earnestly,** περὶ τούτου ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d; ὅπως μή.. [prev. author] “Grg.” 502b.}}
**3** in argument, **contend** or **maintain** that.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 158d}}: with a _negative_, δ. τι μὴ εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.42; δ. ὅτι οὐκ ἀπόλλυται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 106c; ὡς οὐ.. [prev. author] “Prm.” 127e, etc.}}; also δ. τι **carry** a point, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 261a.}}

1266:
** διαμερ-ίζω**,
**divide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15e}}; **distribute,** τὸ ἐπιβάλλον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 27; τοὺς πόνους εἰς ἅπαν τὸ σῶμα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 885a18}}:—_passive_, **to be cut up,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 849d.}}
**II** **part, separate,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 883}}:—_middle_, **divide** or **part among themselves,** {{NT+5th c.AD+|NT.Matt.27.35; πρὸς ἑαυτούς “PAmh.” 2.152.18 (5th-6th c.AD)}}:—_passive_, **to be set at variance,** {{NT|NT.Luke.12.52,53.}}

1267:
** διαμερ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**division,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771d, “POxy.” 12vi 17, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.47, LXX.Eze.48.29 (_plural_), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 10.11.7.}}
**II** **dissension,** {{NT|NT.Luke.12.51.}}

1268:
** διανέμω**,
late _future_ **-νεμήσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.3}}: _aorist_ διένειμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.45, etc.}}; _aorist_ regular -ένειμα, but _infinitive_ διανεμῆσαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus}}**in D.**{{Refs|9.21:}}_perfect_ **-νενέμηκα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.45}}:—**distribute, apportion,** τοῖς μὲν τιμάς, τοῖς δὲ ἀτιμίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 830e, etc.; ἐπὶ τὰ αὑτῶν ἕκαστα ἐκμαγεῖα [prev. author] “Tht.” 194d}}; δ. μέρη **divide into** portions, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 756c, compare “Ti.” 35b; ἡ χώρα κατὰ δώδεκα μέρη διανενέμηται [prev. author] “Lg.” 758e}}; δ. ἑαυτόν **distribute** oneself among friends, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1171a3}}; δ. ἴσον αὑτόν [ὁ Πλοῦτος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 510; ὁ διανέμων}}**the distributor,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1136b26}}; **assign,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 430b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 306b31}}:—_middle_, **divide among themselves,** τὰ κοινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.135; τὴν ἀρχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 523a; δ. τὰ τῶν πλουσίων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281a15; also διανειμάμενοι δίχ᾽ ἑαυτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 153.2}}:—_passive_, δ. εἰς τὸν λαόν **to be spread abroad,** {{NT|NT.Acts.4.17.}}
**II** **set in order, govern,** ἄστυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.261, compare 8.62.}}

1269:
** διανεύω**,
**nod, beckon,** ταῖς κεφαλαῖς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.18}}; τινί to a person, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.12, NT.Luke.1.22, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.25.}}
**II** **bend away from, avoid,** τὰς τῶν ὀργάνων ἐπιβολάς τι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.23.8; ὀργάς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fragmenta - fragment uncertainFragmenta incerta” 27.}}

1271:
** διάνοιᾰ**, ἡ,
_Aeolic dialect_ **διανοιΐα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 1a.1 (nisi to be read δι᾽ ἀνοιΐα[ν])}}, _poetry_ also **διανοίᾱ** _accusative_ to {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1679.29}}:— **thought,** i.e. **intention, purpose,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46, 90, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.35, etc.}}; τῇ διανοίᾳ in the **spirit** of his action, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.219; ὤλοντ᾽ ἀσεβεῖ διανοίᾳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 831 (Lyric poetry); μαινόλις δ. [prev. author] “Supp.” 109 (Lyric poetry); εὔφρονος ἐκ δ. [prev. author] “Ag.” 797 (Lyric poetry), compare “Eu.” 1013 (_anapaest meter_); τοῦ ὑπαπιέναι τὴν διάνοιαν ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9; ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.14; πρός τινι 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.37; ἐπ᾽ ἄλλο τι.. τρέψαι τινὸς τὴν δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275b}}; ἐξ ὅλης τῆς δ. with all one's **heart,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.2.13; ἐχθροὺς τῇ δ. NT.Col.1.21.}}
**2** **thought, notion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.169, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986b10; ἀπὸ τῆς αὐτῆς δ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.210.}}
**II** **process of thinking, thought,** ὁ ἐντὸς τῆς ψυχῆς πρὸς αὑτὴν διάλογος.. ἐπωνομάσθη δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 263d; πᾶσα δ. ἢ πρακτικὴ ἢ ποιητικὴ ἢ θεωρητική 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1025b25; ταχίστη ἡ διανοίας κίνησις [prev. author] “LI” 968a25}}; especially **discursive thought,** opposed to νόησις, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 123.}}
**III** **thinking faculty, intelligence, understanding,** ὡς μεταξύ τι δόξης τε καὶ νοῦ τὴν δ. οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 511d, +others}}; opposed to σῶμα, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 916a, compare “R.” 395b; ἔστιν ὥσπερ τοῦ σώματος καὶ τῆς δ. γῆρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270b40; ἐπιτάττοντος τοῦ νοῦ καὶ λεγούσης τῆς δ. φεύγειν τι ἢ διώκειν [prev. author] “de An.” 433a2; ἔκστασις διανοίας LXX.Deu.28.28.}}
**IV** **thought expressed, meaning of** a word or passage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 205b, “Phdr.” 228d; τὰς τῶν ὀνομάτων δ. [prev. author] “Cra.” 418a; τὴν αὐτὴν ἔχει δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 404a17; ἡ φυσικὴ δ. τοῦ νόμου Aristeas 171}}; so δ., opposed to ῥητόν, **spirit,** opposed to letter, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 2.}}
**V** **intellectual capacity** revealed in speech or action by the characters in drama, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450a6, b11, 1456a34, “Rh.” 1404a19, +others (Rare in Poetry.)}}

1272:
** διαν-οίγω**,
**lay open,** τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 210a; μήτραν NT.Luke.2.23}}:—_passive_, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Gen.3.5, +others, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.86}}; of a dead body, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 507a21.}}
**II** **open so as to connect,** τὸν Ἰνδικὸν καὶ Περσικὸν κόλπον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mu.” 393b3.}}
**III** **reveal, explain,** τὰς γραφάς {{NT+5th c.AD+|NT.Luke.24.32, cf. NT.Act.17.3; τὰ τῶν παλαιῶν ἀπόρρητα 5th-6th c.AD: Aeneas Gazaeus Rhetor “Theophrastus” p.5B.}}

1276:
** διαπεράω**,
**go over** or **across,** ῥοάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1151; πελάγη 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.19; δ. ἐπ᾽ οἶδμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 395 (Lyric poetry)}}; δ. πόλιν **pass through** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1264; δ. Ἑλλάδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 117; δ. εἰς Ἰταλίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 485}}; of Time, δ. τὸν βίον **pass through** life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.7.}}
**b** διαπερᾶν Μολοσσίαν **reign through all** Molossia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1248 codices}}
**2** **pass through, pierce,** κνήμην διεπέρασεν Ἀργεῖον δόρυ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1394}}; **traverse,** ἧπαρ, of a vein, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.8 (compare διαπεραίνω).}}
**3** οἶσθα διαπερῶν **by traversing,** i.e. **by experience,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 994 (Lyric poetry), cf. Scholia}}
**II** **reach, arrive at** a place, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 247.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** _transitive_, **carry over,** ὕδωρ ποταμοῦ σῶμα δ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 151, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 20.1.}}

1277:
** διαπλέω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **δια-πλώω** (which see), **sail through** a strait or gap, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.24, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.10.12}}; sail **across,** Μέγαράδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.17; εἰς Αἴγιναν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 122, etc.: c._accusative_, δ. τὸ πέλαγος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.206d, “IG” 14.1976: _metaphorically_, δ. βίον}} **sail through** life, **make** life's **voyage,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85d.}}
**2** **flow through, pass,** τὰ ψαμμία σὺν τοῖσι οὔροισι δ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.3.}}

1278:
** διαπον-έω**,
**work out with labour, elaborate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.85}}; **cultivate, practise,** δ. γράμματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 810b, compare “R.” 535c; τὰ πρὸς ἀγῶνας συντείνοντα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1341a11}}; **exercise,** σώματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.10; σώματα δρόμοις καὶ πάλαις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 14; αὑτὸν [prev. author] “Dem.” 5; στρατόν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 43; τοὺς νέους 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 18:}} —_middle_, διαπονεῖσθαι ἐπιτηδεύματα, τέχνας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 846d, compare “Phdr.” 273e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.33}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 118c, “Ep.” 326d, etc.; οἴκου.. οὐχ ὡς τὰ πρόσθ᾽ ἄριστα διαπονουμένου}}**managed, governed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 19}}; διαπονηθῆναι τὴν μουσικήν **to be taught** it **thoroughly,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 4; διαπεπονημένοι}}**veterans,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.7; ὄψων.. περιττῶς διαπεπονημένων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 40.}}
**2** **till** or **cultivate completely,** χώραν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.45.7}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|“Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 12.5.}}
**3** _passive_, **to be worn out, troubled,** {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Acts.4.2, 16.18, “POxy.” 743.22 (1st c.BC).}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to work hard,** δ. τῇ διανοίᾳ καὶ τῷ σώματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1339a8, cf. Aristeas 92; περὶ τὸ σῶμα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1178a26; περὶ τὰ δημιουργικὰ τεχνήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 846d}}: with _infinitive_, δ. πᾶν ἰσόρροπον ποιεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.17}}: —_middle_, δ. τὸ πᾶσαν πίστιν λαβεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966c; δ. περὶ τὸν γόνον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 759b1}}; οἱ διαπονούμενοι **the hard-working, hardy,** opposed to ἄπονοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.8}}; **to be hard-worked,** of hounds, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Cynegeticus” 32.1, +others}}

1279:
** διαπορ-εύω**,
**carry over, conduct through,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.18.}}
**II** mostly _middle_, with _aorist_ _passive_ διεπορεύθην:—**pass across,** ἐς Εὔβοιαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33}}: with _accusative_, **go through,** Πελοπόννησον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.52; χώραν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.3; δ. τὰς ὁδούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 845a; στόμα δι᾽ οὗ μέλη τε καὶ ἔπη δ. 1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus “Vit.Eur.fragment” 39xx14}}; δ. γραμμήν **travel along** a line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1174b1, compare 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 12: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.107, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 640b15}}; οἱ διαπορευόμενοι the **passers-by,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 32.10.}}
**II.2** **go through, detail,** εὐεργεσίας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.26.2}}; **perform,** τὰς κρούσεις {{Refs|[prev. author] 30.22.5.}}
**II.3** of Time, **elapse**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1116.11, +others (1st c.BC).}}

1280:
** διᾰπορ-έω**,
**to be quite at a loss, to be in doubt** or **difficulty,** τί χρὴ δρᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777c; ἐπὶ τοῖς συμβαίνουσι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.71.5}}: in _aorist_ _passive_, διηπορήθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.34}}: _perfect_ _passive_, διηπορημένος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 25}}:—_middle_, δ. ὑπ᾽ αἰσχύνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237a.}}
**2** **to be in want,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1353a26.}}
**II** **go through all the** ἀπορίαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1276b36, +others: but}}
**II.2** commonly only a stronger form of ἀπορέω, **raise an** ἀπορία, **start a difficulty,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “EN” 1096a11; ἔστι δὲ τοῖς εὐπορῆσαι βουλομένοις προὔργου τὸ διαπορῆσαι καλῶς [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 995a28; πὲρί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.20.2, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 21; εἰ.. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 21}}:—_middle_, διαπορεῖσθαί τι περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217a}}:—_passive_, **to be matter of doubt** or **discussion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 250e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1086a19, +others; τὸ διαπορούμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 799e; τὸ διαπορεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a35; τὸ διαπορηθέν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1282b8: _impersonal_, διαπορεῖται περί τινος}} **a question arises** about.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 631b2.}}

1281:
** διαπραγμᾰτεύομαι**,
**discuss** or **examine thoroughly,** τοῦτον τὸν λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77d; τὴν αἰτίαν [prev. work] 95e.}}
**II** **gain by trading,** {{NT|NT.Luke.19.15.}}
**III** **accomplish,** τι πρὸς τοὺς θεούς {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 5.16.}}

1282:
** δια-πρίω** [ρῑ],
**saw through, saw asunder,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 768, conjecture in 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 4.2 (compare διαπίμπρημι)}}; **split,** κύμινον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 312a}}:— _passive_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 21; -πεπρισμένα [ξύλα] “SIG” 2587.304; διαπεπρισμένα ἡμίσε᾽.. ὡσπερεὶ τὰ σύμβολα 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 70: _metaphorically_, διεπρίοντο ταῖς καρδίαις NT.Act.7.54, compare 5.33; also εἰς πλείω δ. τὴν Παλαιστίνην 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 334.}}
**II** δ. τοὺς ὀδόντας **gnash** the teeth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cal.” 24.}}
**III** διαπρίεται· διαγοράζει, μαίνεται, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1283:
** διαρπ-άζω**,
_future_ **-άσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336b}}, later **-άσω** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 55}}:— **tear in pieces,** [λύκοι] αἶψα διαρπάζουσι [ἄρνας] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.355}}; of the wind, **carry away, efface,** τὰ ἴχνη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.2; τείχη διηρπασμένα}}**dismantled,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Ath.**279a.
**II** **spoil, plunder,** πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, etc.}}:—_middle_, Συρίαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.9.1.}}
**II.2** **seize as plunder,** χρήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.6, 19.40, “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 13.3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281a25, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 274b; τἀν τῇ Βοιωτίᾳ διαρπασθησόμεν᾽ ὑπὸ τοῦ πολέμου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.213, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.41, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.36.}}
**II.3** **snatch from,** θηρείων παῖδα γενείων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 48.290.}}

1286:
** δια-σείω**,
**shake violently,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.6, uncertain in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 64.2; τι εἰς ἀταξίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85e, compare 88a; τὴν κεφαλήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.435c: with _dative_, δ. τοῖν χεροῖν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae 50}}; δ. τῇ οὐρᾷ **to keep wagging** the tail, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.15}}:—_middle_, **shake** people **off, shake oneself free,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.56.}}
**2** **confound, throw into confusion,** τὰ τῶν Ἀθηναίων φρονήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109; τοὺς ἀκούοντας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.45.2}}; **intimidate, oppress,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.26.4, compare “OGI” 519.14 (_passive_)}}; **browbeat,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTaur. 1viii13(2nd c.BC)}}; **extort money by intimidation from** a person, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 15.37 (2nd c.BC), NT.Luke.3.14, etc.: with _genitive_, “PTeb.” 41.10 (2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 284.5 (1st c.AD).}}
**3** of political affairs, **throw into confusion,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 10.}}
**4** **stir up,** in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 29.}}
**5** **sound, take the measure of,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.580d,704d.}}

1288:
** δια-σπάω**,
_future_ -σπάσομαι [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 477, “Ec.” 1076}} also **-σπάσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236:}}_aorist_ -έσπᾰσα, _middle_ **-εσπασάμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1126, “Ba.” 339, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 68}}: _perfect_ -έσπᾰκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus “Oxy.” 853i15:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ -εσπάσθην: _perfect_ -έσπασμαι (see. below):—**tear asunder,** τοὺς ἄνδρας κρεοργηδὸν δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus and 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [same places], etc.; ἐμὲ καὶ τὸν ἄνδρα δ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.45}}; δ. τὸ σταύρωμα **to break through** or **tear down** the palisade, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 4.4.10}}; δ. τὴν γέφυραν, τὸ ἔδαφος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.55.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 5}}; **break up**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 364.10 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, διασπᾶν τὴν σύμπνοιαν τοῦ παντός {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21.λ}}:—_passive_, διέσπασται μελέων φύσις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 63; τὸ Ἀττικὸν [ἔθνος].. διεσπασμένον ὑπὸ Πεισιστράτου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; μόνον οὐ διεσπάσθην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.5}}; δ. ἀπὸ τῶν φίλων **to be torn away** from.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1386a10.}}
**2** in military sense, **separate** part of an army from the rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.19}}; of army and fleet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.236}}; δ. τὰς φάλαγγας **break** them **up,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303b13:}} —_passive_, στράτευμα διεσπασμένον an army **scattered and in disorder,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.98, compare 7.44}}; of a fleet, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.104; τῷ διεσπάσθαι τὰς δυνάμεις}}**to be widely scattered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.9.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **pull different ways,** πόλεις **distract** states, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 875a; τὰς πολιτείας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.48; τοὺς νόμους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.25}}; διέσπακε τὴν ἱστορίαν **has broken the continuity of** the narrative, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus [same place]:}} —_passive_, διασπώμενος **distracted,** πρὸς τοσαύτας ὑπηρεσίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 24.1; ὑπὸ τῶν λόγων [prev. author] “Icar.” 23.}}

1290:
** διασπορ-ά**, ἡ,
(διασπείρω) **scattering, dispersion,** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1105a, LXX.Jer.15.7; δ. ψυχική 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.426.}}
**2** collectively, ={οἱ διεσπαρμένοι}, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.28.25, NT.John.7.35: _plural_, LXX.Psa.146(147).2.}}

1291:
** διαστέλλω**,
**put asunder, expand, separate,** συνεσταλμένα δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Off.” 11; τὸν ἀέρα ταῖς πτέρυξιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 713a12; τινὰς ξίφει 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.2.2}}; δ. τι ταῖς ὄνυξι **to tear** it **open,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 36}}:—_passive_, **to be dilated,** of the lungs or heart, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 800b2, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.657, +others; διασταλέντα τὰ ὑγρά}}**being dispersed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 891a2}}; διασταλήτω πᾶσα σκοτία **let** all darkness **be dispelled,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.2472.}}
**2** **divide,** δίχα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 265e}}; **distinguish,** τοὺς Τρῶας τῶν Δαρδάνων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” “Oxy.” 1086.115; τῷ τόνῳ “POxy.” 1012 “fragment” 16.5 (_passive_).}}
**2.b** **define precisely,** τὰ λεγόμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 295d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 134b22, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.50S.}}; _intransitive_, ὅρασις διαστέλλουσα **distinct** vision, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.3.1}}; also, **to be distinctive,** opposed to ἀπόλυτον εἶναι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 39.1}}:—_middle_, δ. περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268b32, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 8: with _accusative_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 535b}}:—_passive_, διεσταλμένος **definite, determinate,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 37.7, +others}}
**3** **command expressly, give express orders,** ῥητῶς ὑπέρ τινος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.23.5; ἐπιτακτικῶς δ. περί τινων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 28.15}}:—_middle_, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Josh.1.19, +others, “PHal.” 7.6 (3rd c.BC), NT.Mark.5.43, etc.}}
**4** **pronounce,** χείλεσι {{LXX|LXX.Lev.5.4.}}
**5** **give orders in writing**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 111.6 (2nd c.BC):}} —more frequently in _middle_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 11.23 (2nd c.BC), +others}}
**6** **pay, render,** especially in kind, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 88.5 (2nd c.AD), +others}}; **make an order for payment,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Ostr. 1164 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; **discharge** a vow, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.22.21.}}
**7** **set apart,** τινὰ ἑαυτῷ{{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.3Ki.8.53; τὴν ἱερὰν γῆν “PRev.Laws” 36.7 (3rd c.BC),compare “PTeb.” 74.2 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** _intransitive_, **differ,** πρός τινα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.47.11.}}

1292:
** διάστημα**, ατος, τό,
(διαστῆναι) **interval,** frequently in Music, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531a, 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.4 M., +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 922b6, 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.57}}; of Time, δ. τετραετές {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.1.1: generally, ἐκ μεγάλων δ. κινεῖσθαι 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 191; δ. μεταξὺ κόσμων 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.37U.}}; **distance,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.8,9.}}
**b** Geometry texts, **radius,** κέντρῳ τῷ Α, διαστήματι τῷ ΑΒ, γεγράφθω κύκλος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.1, compare 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 52.14}}; of a sphere, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 6.}}
**c** **aperture,** ἀγγεῖον ἔχον δ. μέγα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 787b4; ἐκ πολλοῦ δ. [prev. author] “Aud.” 800a36}}; τὰ δ. τῆς χειρὸς τῶν δακτύλων the **spaces** between the fingers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.35.}}
**2** in Medicine texts, **diastasis,** of bones, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 23 (_plural_), compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).887.}}
**3** **difference,** τῶν ἡδονῶν μεγάλα τὰ δ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.22.}}
**4** **ratio,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 202a18.}}
**5** in Aristotle's symbolism, **conjunction of two terms, APr.**{{Refs|42b10, “APo.” 82b7, +others}}
**6** **extension, dimension,** χρόνος κινήσεως δ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.26, cf. Chrysipp.[prev. work]2.164, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 389}}; of Space, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 209a4, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.4.2}}; ὧν πρότερον διάστημα ἐνειστήκει whose **extension** (i.e. **surface**) it (the εἴδωλον) formerly occupied, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 2.3.}}
**II** **distinction** of style, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.2.}}

1293:
** διαστολ-ή**, ἡ,
(διαστέλλω) **drawing asunder, dilatation,** of the lungs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 800a35}}; of the heart, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.597}}; of the pulse, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.736, +others}}; δ. χειλέων **parting** of the lips, i.e. **utterance,** {{LXX|LXX.Num.30.7.}}
**b** **separation,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.16.3}}; **notch** or **nick,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 1}}; **boundary, fence,** {{Refs|Tab.Heracl. 2.46}}; **fencing off,** τῆς γῆς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.40.25 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **distinction,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.158, LXX.Exo.8.23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 28.7, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 123, NT.Rom.3.22; ἁγίων καὶ βεβήλων 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.159}}; μετὰ διαστολῆς προενεχθέντα with **discrimination,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus 1014.48 F.}}; **detailed statement** or **explanation,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.15.6; ἀξίους μνήμης καὶ δ. [prev. author] 16.14.2, compare “SIG” 284.11 (from Chios), 1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 3}}; **specification** of items in an account, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PRyl.” 65.17 (1st c.BC)}}; **article** in a contract, etc., {{Refs|“PTheb.Bank” 6.8, 7.7.}}
**3** **command, injunction, order,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Num.19.2, +others, “PTeb.” 24.45 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**4** **payment**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 485.26, “PTeb.” 363.1 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** Grammars, **comma** (as in ὅ, τι), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 629.}}
**II.b** **distinction,** γενῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 11.28, +others}}
**II.c** **opposition,** πρός τι {{Refs|[prev. work] 41.24.}}
**II.2** in Music, **distinctness,** of notes, {{NT|NT.1Cor.14.7.}}

1294:
** διαστρέφω**,
**turn different ways, twist about,** τὰ σώματα, as in the dance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 7.3}}; δ. τὸ πρόσωπον **to distort** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.535a}}:—mostly _passive_, **to be distorted** or **twisted,** of the eyes, limbs, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.49; ἡ ῥὶς δ. [prev. author] “Art.” 38; μέλη διεστραμμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 524c}}; **to be warped,** τὰ διεστραμμένα τῶν ξύλων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109b6}}: also of persons, **to have one's eyes distorted,** or **to have one's neck twisted** (Scholl. give both interpretations), εὐδαιμονίζω δ᾽ εἰ διαστραφήσομα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 175; so ἀπολαύσομαί τί γ᾽ εἰ δ. [prev. author] “Av.” 177}}; of the eyes, διεστράφην ἰδών {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 15; τὰ ὄμματα διαστρέφεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 960a13; without ὄμματα, [prev. work] 9, compare 957b7}}; ὁ διεστραμμένος, opposed to ὁ τυφλός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 276.3}}; διεστρ. τοὺς πόδας **with** the feet **twisted,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.18.1, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 896b5: of torture, τῇ κλίμακι διαστρέφονται “Comica Adespota” 422}}; διεστράφησαν τὸν στόμαχον **had** their stomachs **turned,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.190d.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **distort, pervert,** [τρόπον χρηστόν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 597; τοὺς νόμους 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.4; τὸν δικαστήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a24; ὑπόληψιν [prev. author] “EN” 1140b14}}; τῶν διαστρεφόντων (i.e. παθῶν) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.32 O.}}; διαστρέψαντες τἀληθῆ **having misrepresented** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 46.2}}:—_passive_, διαστραφῆναι τὴν διάνοιαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit. Auct.” 24; γενεὰ διεστραμμένη}}**perverse,** {{LXX|LXX.Deu.32.5.}}
**II** **turn aside, divert,** ἴχνος τὸ πρόσθεν φρενῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1017.}}
**III** obscene meaning, ={βινεῖν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 7 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

1295:
** διασῴζω**,
**preserve through** a danger, of persons, Ἀπόλλωνα δ. κατακρύψασα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.156; δ. πόλιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 783; δ. τινὰ ἐκ κινδύνων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.23}}:—_middle_, **save for oneself,** τὰ πλείστου ἄξια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.28}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 22d}}; **come safe through,** τοὺς διασωθέντας {{NT|[prev. author] “R.” 540a, cf. NT.1Pet.3.20, etc.}}; διασῴζεσθαι ἐς.. or πρός.. **to come safe** to a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.113, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.5, etc.}}; **recover** from illness, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 2.10.2.}}
**II** of things, **preserve, maintain,** ἀνδρὶ τἀμὰ δ. λέχη.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 65}}; **keep in memory,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.22; δ. πίστιν τινί [prev. author] “HG” 7.2.17; δ. τὸν πρῶτον λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 189b1; τὰ παλαιά 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.63}}:—_middle_, **preserve for oneself, retain,** εὐδαιμονίαν, εὐτυχίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39, 5.16; δόξαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.69; τὴν τῶν Μήδων μαλακίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.15.}}

1299:
** διατάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **διατάττω**, _perfect_ διατέτᾰχα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1151.6 (1st c.BC), probably in “OGI” 326.27 (from Teos)}}:—**appoint** or **ordain severally, dispose,** εὖ δὲ ἕκαστα ἀθανάτοις διέταξε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 74; ἀνθρώποισι νόμον δ. [prev. author] “Op.” 276}}; **appoint to separate offices,** δ. τοὺς μὲν οἰκίας οἰκοδομέειν, τοὺς δὲ δορυφόρους εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114; δ. τι εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 45b; τίνας εἶναι χρεὼν τῶν ἐπιστημῶν.. ἡ πολιτικὴ δ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094b1}}: _absolutely_, **make arrangements,** πρὸς τὸ συμπῖπτον ἀεὶ δ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.16}}:—_middle_, **arrange for oneself, classify,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271b; τινὶ περί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.21.1}}; **undertake, pledge oneself,** πρός τινα with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. work] 14.11}}; also in _active_ sense, περὶ θυσιῶν {{Refs|“OGI” 331.53 (Pergam.)}}:—_passive_, **to be appointed, constituted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 932a}}; παρὰ τὰ -τεταγμένα contrary to **orders**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1022.17 (2nd c.AD)}}: with _infinitive_, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.110: with _accusative_, δ. γῆν}}**to be appointed to cultivate,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|POxy. 899.22 (200 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator).}}
**2** especially **draw up** an army, **set in array,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.107, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.103; διέταξε χωρὶς ἑκάστους εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103}}:—_middle_, διαταξάμενοι **posting themselves in battle-order,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 360, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.20}}:—_perfect_ _passive_, διατετάχθαι **to be in battleorder,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.178, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.31; διετέτακτο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.112 (but in middle sense, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.5.4).}}
**II** _middle_, **make teslamentary dispositions,** περί τινος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1129a}}; **order by will,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.133 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; **bequeath**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1151.6 (1st c.BC)}}:—_passive_, **to be bequeathed,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PFay. 97.13 (1st c.AD).}}
